/WE MUST OUTPUT THE ESCAPE SEQUENCE EVEN THOUGH DD IS CHST.
/THEREFORE WE CANNOT USE PRESCS.

PGCHAR,	XX								/A234
	TAD	(XSESCK)	/PUT OUT ESCAPE				/A234
	DCA	XSADX	/STRING FOR THE					/A234
	JMS	ESCST	/ESCAPE KEY					/A234
	JMS	PLNXT	/GET NEXT CHARACTER				/A234
	NOP		/CAN'T FAIL					/A234
	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET CHARACTER					/A234
	JMP I	PGCHAR

XSESCK,	ESC;	"[&177;	"2&177;	"3&177;	"~&177+4000/ESCAPE SEQUENCE FOR CHST
/------------------------

	PAGE

OVTYPE=OLC
OVNUM=OVNUM+1
OVADR=OVADR+400
OVRLCA=PROVA+OVTYPE
DL=DLOPRO+OVNUM		/DISK BLOCK OF THIS OVERLAY
FIELD OVNUM%20+3

RELOC
*OVADR
RELOC OVRLCA

	-OVNUM
OVDPL2=.-OVRLCA
OBDPL2=OVTYPE+OVNUM

/IN PRINTING TO DRAFT PRINTERS, VERY LITTLE IS PRESUMED ABOUT THE
/ATTACHED PRINTER.  WE ASSUME THAT CARRIAGE RETURN RETURNS THE
/CARRIAGE BUT DOES NOT IMPLY A LINE FEED.  BACKSPACE IS USED ONLY
/TO IMPLEMENT DEAD KEY SEQUENCES.  THUS, IN GENERAL, THIS ROUTINE
/USES A MULTIPLE PASS STRATEGY TO PRINT THE LINE.  COMMENTS AT
/DPLIN3 FURTHER EXPLAIN THE MULTIPLE PASS STRATEGY


/RESET ALL POINTERS. THE UPPER FIVE (5) BITS OF EACH WORD
/IN THE LINE HAVE BEEN PROCESSED.
/THIS IS THE START OF THE LINE.

DPLIN2,	JMP	LPRSTP	/RESET THE POINTERS, RETURN TO DPLIN3

/THIS INNER LOOP PROCESSES EACH CHARACTER IN THE LINE, AND ACTS
/ACCORDING TO EACH CHARACTER'S MODE BITS.

/A) IF NONE OF A CHARACTER'S MODE BITS ARE SET, THIS IS THE LAST PASS DURING
/WHICH IT WILL PRINT.  IT IS REPLACED IN THE BUFFER BY A SPACE.


/B) IF A CHARACTER IS UNDERLINED, AN UNDERSCORE IS PRINTED INSTEAD
/OF THE CHARACTER UNTIL THE PROPER UNDERLINE DARKNESS IS ACHIEVED.
/THEN THE UNDERLINE ATTRIBUTE IS DELETED AND THE CHARACTER ITSELF
/PRINTS NORMALLY ON SUBSEQUENT PASSES.

/C) THE DARKNESS REQUIRED WILL DETERMINE HOW MANY PASSES THROUGH THE LINE
/WILL BE DONE. THE HIT BITS (BITS 1&2 OF THE WORD) DETERMINE THE PASSES.
/00= ONE PASS, 01= TWO PASSES, 10= THREE PASSES, 11= FOUR PASSES.

/D) IF A CHARACTER IS PART OF A DEAD KEY SEQUENCE, EACH CHARACTER
/IN THE SEQUENCE IS TREATED SEPERATELY.  IF A CHARACTER IN A DEAD KEY
/SEQUENCE IS REPLACED WITH A SPACE AND THAT IS NOT THE LAST PASS,
/WE PAY A SMALL SPEED PENALTY.

/IF A CHARACTER FALLS INTO ANY CATEGORY OTHER THAN A), ANOTHER PASS IS
/NECESSARY.  EVENTUALLY, ALL CHARACTERS WILL FALL INTO CATEGORY A) AND
/THE LINE WILL BE COMPLETED.


DPLIN3,	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET THE FIRST CHARACTER IN ITS POSITION
	SNA		/FLUSH NULL CHARACTERS. THEY CAN'T BE PROCESSED.
	JMP	DPLIN9	/GET NEXT CHARACTER

/WE SAVE THE CHARACTER WITHOUT MODE BITS IN THE AC

	AND	(4177)	/MASK OUT THE MODE BITS
	MQL		/SAVE THE MODE-BIT-LESS CHARACTER IN THE MQ

/WE NOW MUST STRIP ALL THE ATTRIBUTES (AFTER ALL THIS WORK!)		/A214
/IF WE ARE PRINTING TO A REAL HOST. (AS OPPOSED TO A PRINTER		/A214
/CONNECTED TO THE HOST PORT).						/A214

	TAD	PRQPNT		/GET DOCUMENT DESTINATION		/A214
	TAD	(-DHOST2)	/					/A214
	SZA CLA			/SKIP IF A REAL HOST			/A214
	JMP	DPLIN4		/REAL PRINTER				/A214
	MQA			/GET MODELESS CHR			/A214
	DCA I	PLPTR		/SAVE BACK IN THE BUFFER		/A214

/NOW CHECK FOR THE CODE 21, 22, OR 23. THIS IS THE SEQUENCE FOR
/PRINTING A TECHNICAL CHARACTER IN ANOTHER CHARACTER SET. IT ALSO CHANGES TO
/G2 OR G3. THE CHARACTER STRING WILL BE TWO CHARACTERS. THE FIRST WILL BE THE
/CONTROL CHARACTER (21,22,OR 23), THE NEXT WILL BE THE CHARACTER OR THE "G"
/SET DESIRED. NOTE THAT THIS TWO CHARACTER STRING IS NOT CALLED A "G" STRING
/BUT IT COULD BE IF YOU SO DESIRE. THE CHARACTER ATTRIBUTES WILL ALSO BE
/ON THE CONTROL CHARACTER.

DPLIN4,	TAD I	PLPTR	/NOW CHECK TO SEE IF THE CHARACTER IS A CONTROL
	AND	P177	/CHARACTER THAT PRECEDES THE TECHNICAL CHARACTER.
	TAD	(-23)
	SNA		/IS IT 23?
	JMP	DPLI4H	/YES
	IAC
	SNA		/IS IT 22?
	JMP	DPLI4C	/IS 22 PROCESS AND DON'T CONTRIBUTE TO JUSTIFICATION
	IAC
	SNA CLA		/IS IT 21?
	JMP	DPLI4E	/YES

	TAD I	PLPTR	/NOT A CONTROL CHARACTER, GET IT BACK AND CONTINUE

/PAPER MOVEMENT CHECK AREA.
/NOW CHECK TO SEE IF SUPERSCRIPT OR SUBSCRIPT IS REQUIRED. THIS WILL	/A222
/MOVING THE PAPER UP OR DOWN AS REQUIRED PRIOR TO PRINTING THE CHARACTER/A222

	AND	(0400)	/MASK OUT ALL BUT THE SUPER/SUB BIT.		/A222
	SNA CLA		/?						/A222
	JMP	DPLI4A	/NO SUPER OR SUB BIT, JUST PRINT THE CHARACTER	/A222
	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET CHARACTER BACK
	AND	(0200)	/TEST FOR SUPERSCRIPT				/A222
	SNA CLA								/A222
	JMP	LPSUPR	/POSITION TO SUPERSCRIPT AND RETURN TO DPLIN5	/A222
	JMP	LPSUB	/POSITION TO SUBSCRIPT AND RETURN TO DPLIN5	/A222

/NO SUPER OR SUB IN CHARACTER ATTRIBUTES

DPLI4A,	TAD	CURPOS	/ARE WE POSITIONED CORRECTLY?
	SNA
	JMP	DPLIN5	/YES

	SPA		/NO, DO WE HAVE TO GO UP OR DOWN?
	JMP	DPLI4B	/IS NEGATIVE, GO UP

	JMS	MOVEDN	/IS UP, GO DOWN
	JMP	DPLIN5	/DONE

DPLI4B,	JMS	MOVEUP	/WE ARE DOWN, MOVE UP.

/CHECK TO SEE IF UNDERSCORE

DPLIN5,	CLA
	JMS	CFUNDL	/CHECK FOR UNDERLINE ATTRIBUTES
	JMP	PUNDL	/RETURN 1: YES, GO TO PUNDL AND RETURN TO DPLI5D
DPLI5A,	TAD I	PLPTR	/RETURN 2: NO UNDL, GET CHARACTER
	AND	(2000)	/SEE IF BOLD BIT SET.
	DCA	PRBIC	/SET FLAG IN LPBI ROUTINE.
	TAD	PRBIF	/CHECK TO SEE IF ANOTHER MEANING FOR BOLD BIT.
	SZA CLA
	JMS	LPBI	/YES, CHANGE FONT OR CHARACTER SETS.
DPLI5B,	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET THE CHR BACK

/WE TEST TO SEE IF THE CODE IS THAT OF THE REQUIRED SPACE RQSP		/A216
/IF SO, WE PRINT THE SPACE CODE (40) INSTEAD OF THE REQUIRED SPACE (177)/A216
/CODE. THIS IS BECAUSE THE REQUIRED SPACE CODE IS JUST A PHONY CODE TO	/A216
/BE REPLACED AT THIS TIME SO THAT IT CANNOT CONTRIBUTE TO JUSTIFICATION	/A216

	AND	P177	/BIT 0 MAY OR MAY NOT BE SET
	TAD	(-RQSP)	/SUBTRACT REQUIRED SPACE CODE (177)		/A216
	SZA CLA		/SKIP IF REQUIRED SPACE				/A216
	JMP	DPLI5C	/NOT A REQUIRED SPACE
	TAD	(SPC)	/IT IS A REQUIRED SPACE.
	JMP	DPLI5E	/PRINT AN ASCII 40 (SPACE).

DPLI5C,	JMS	PRSO	/SEE IF PRINT SHIFT OUT (SO)

DPLI5D,	MQA
DPLI5E,	JMS	PRCHAR	/PRINT IT AFTER ALL THE FINAGLEING.
	CLA
	JMS	PRSI	/DO WE PRINT SHIFT IN (SI)?


/NOW WE SEE IF ANY JUSTIFICATION IS NECESSARY.  ALL SPACES IN THE
/JUSTIFIABLE PORTION OF THE LINE ARE MARKED WITH BIT 0 SET.  AN EXTRA
/PLSS UNITS ARE ADDED TO EACH SUCH SPACE.  IN ADDITION, PLSSC IS
/INCREMENTED EACH TIME.  AS LONG AS IT REMAINS NEGATIVE OR ZERO, AN
/EXTRA UNIT IS ADDED TO EACH SPACE.  SINCE ONLY UNITS IN MULTIPLES OF
/SPACES ARE OUTPUT IN LPMOVE, LEFTOVER UNITS ARE RETAINED UNTIL THE
/NEXT SPACE.
	
/FIRST DETERMINE IF THE CHARACTER JUST OUTPUT WAS A JUSTIFIABLE SPACE.

	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET THE CHARACTER
	SMA CLA		/WAS IT A JUSTIFIABLE CHARACTER--CONSIDER AC0
	JMP	DPLIN8	/IT WASN'T--NO JUSTIFICATION

/Next determine how much space to add.  We increment PLSSC each time.  As soon
/as it reaches zero, we now longer use one additional unit.

	ISZ	PLSSC	/Increment the remainder counter
	NOP		/In case it skips

	TAD	PLSSC	/See if the remainder counter is still negative
	SPA CLA
	AC0001		/Yes--use one additional unit

	TAD	PLSS	/Recover the extra space sizing

/WE NOW HAVE THE AMOUNT OF EXTRA SPACING REQUIRED IN THE AC.  LPMOVE PADS
/OUT AS MUCH OF THE EXTRA SPACE AS IT CAN BY PRINTING THE CHARACTER IN THE
/MQ.

	JMS	LPMOVE	/DO THE MOTION

	JMP	DPLIN8
/----------------
	PAGE

/HERE WE CHECK TO SEE IF THE CHARACTER IS THE LAST DARKNESS REQUIRED.
/WE REPLACE IT WITH A SPACE IF ALL HITS BITS ON THE CHARACTER ARE ZERO.
/IF THE CHARACTER IS AN 21,22,OR 23, THEN REPLACE WITH A NULL IF HIT BITS =0.
/BOLD (BIT 1) AND DARK (BIT 2) ARE DECREMENTED AND ANOTHER PASS FLAG SET.
/ALSO THE BOLD INTERPRETATION FLAG IS CHECKED IF THE HIT BITS ARE NOT 0.
/THIS IS THE FLAG THAT CHANGES THE MEANING OF THE BOLD BIT. IF PRBIF SET, THEN
/INTERPRET THE BOLD BIT AS CHANGE FONT OR CHARACTER SET FOR THAT CHARACTER ONLY.
/THIS IS SO YOU CAN PRINT WITH AN ACCENT OR UMLAUT OVER THE CHARACTER FROM
/ANOTHER FONT. DARK BIT STILL MEANS THE SAME.
/ALSO HERE IF CONTROL CHARACTER FOR TECH CHAR AND A G SET CHANGE WAS MADE.

DPLIN8,	CLA
	TAD	PPUNDL	/DID I PRINT AN UNDERLINE?
	SZA CLA
	JMP	DPLI9A	/YES, THEREFORE DON'T DECREMENT HIT COUNTS
	TAD I	PLPTR	/ARE THE HIT BITS 0?
	AND	(3000)	/MASK THEM.
	SZA		/IF 0, THEN REPLACE WITH A SPACE.
	JMP	DPLI8A	/DON'T REPLACE WITH A SPACE YET.

/SEE IF 21,22, OR 23

	TAD I	PLPTR
	AND	P177
	TAD	(-23)
	SZA
	IAC
	SZA
	IAC
	SNA CLA
	JMP	DPLI8C	/PUT IN A NULL BECAUSE IT IS A 21, 22, OR 23

	TAD I	PLPTR
	SMA		/IS IT A JUSTIFIABLE SPACE?
	JMP	DPLI8B	/NO


DPLI8C,	DCA I	PLPTR	/NULL OUT 21, 22, OR 23 OR RESAVE JUSTIFIABLE SPACE
	ISZ	TCHBLD	/BUMP THE COUNTER FOR TECH CHAR			/A238
	NOP								/A238
	JMP	DPLIN9	/GET THE NEXT CHARACTER AND GO PRINT IT MAYBE.


DPLI8B,	CLA
	TAD	(SPC)
	JMP	DPLI8C

/HERE IF SOME HIT BIT SET.

DPLI8A,	DCA	T1	/SAVE THE HIT BITS. WE NOW CHECK 2 WAYS TO INTERPRET.

	TAD	TCHBLD	/TCHBLD IS A FLAG INITIALLY SET TO -2
			/CHECK IF TECHNICAL CHARACTER. IF SO, NO PRBIF.
	SMA CLA		/IS NEXT CHARACTER A TECH CHARACTER?
	JMP	DPLI8I	/THIS IS NOT A TECH CHARACTER, BUT IT MIGHT BE PRBIF.
	ISZ	TCHBLD	/IT IS A CODE FOR THE TECH CHARACTER.
	NOP
	JMP	DPLI8D	/DECREMENT HIT BITS NORMALLY. IT IS A CONTROL CHARACTER
			/OR A TECH CHARACTER. PRBIF DOES NOT APPLY.
DPLI8I,	TAD	PRBIF	/IS THE BOLD BIT USED TO SELECT ANOTHER FONT?
	SNA CLA
	JMP	DPLI8D	/NO CONTINUE AS BEFORE.

/HERE IF BOLD INTERPRETATION FLAG SET AND SOME HIT BITS SET.

	TAD	T1	/YES, NOW SEE IF ANOTHER PASS NEEDED IF DARK BIT SET.
	AND	(2000)	/FIRST CHECK BOLD BIT.
	SNA CLA
	JMP	DPLI8D	/PRBIF, NO BOLD JUST DARK. JUST DECREMENT NORMALLY.

DPLI8G,	TAD	T1	/IS BOLD AND PRBIF. PRESERVE BOLD, DECREMENT DARK.
	AND	(1000)	/GET DARK BIT
	SNA CLA
	JMP	DPLI8F	/NO DARK, BOLD SET, PRBIF SET, REPLACE WITH SPACE (040)
	TAD I	PLPTR	/YES CLEAR DARK, AND SET UP FOR ANOTHER PASS WITH BOLD SET.
	AND	(6777)	/CLEAR DARK BIT, AND PRINT ANOTHER PASS.
	JMP	DPLI8E

DPLI8D,	TAD	T1	/GET BOLD AND DARK BACK
	TAD	(3000)	/DECREMENT THEM BY ADDING -1 IN BITS 1&2
	AND	(3000)	/DON'T CARRY INTO JUSTIFIABLE BIT.
	DCA	T1
	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET CHARACTER
	AND	(4777)	/MASK OUT HIT BITS.
	TAD	T1
DPLI8E,	DCA I	PLPTR	/NOW SET FLAG FOR ANOTHER PASS.
DPLI9A,	CLA
	DCA	PPUNDL
	ISZ	LPPASS	/SET TO DO ANOTHER PASS THROUGH THE LINE.
DPLIN9,	JMS	PLNXT	/POINT AT THE NEXT CHARACTER
	JMP	DPLI10	/NO MORE CHARACTERS--END OF THE LINE
	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET THE NEXT CHARACTER
	TAD	(-BS)	/IS IT A BACKSPACE?
	SZA CLA
	JMP	DPLIN3	/NO--THIS IS A DESIRED CHARACTER

/WE HAVE FOUND A BACKSPACE; OUTPUT IT & DO NEXT CHR.

	TAD I	PLPTR
	JMS	PRCHAR
	JMP	DPLIN9

/HERE IF, PRBIF SET, BOLD SET, DARK =0, AND CHARACTER PRINTED.
/CLEAR BOLD BIT, SET UP TO REPLACE WITH SPACE AND GET NEXT CHARACTER.

DPLI8F,	TAD I	PLPTR
	AND	(4177)	/PRESERVE BIT 0 AND CHARACTER
	DCA I	PLPTR
	JMP	DPLIN8	/NOW GO REPROCESS WITH THE APPEARANCE OF ALL HIT BITS 0.

/HERE TO SEE IF WE NEED TO HAVE TO REPOSITION THE PAPER PRIOR TO THE
/CARRIAGE RETURN. IT MIGHT HAVE BEEN SUPER OR SUB.

DPLI10,	TAD	CURPOS	/THIS IS TO SEE WHERE THE LINE IS BEFORE THE END/A222
	SNA		/OF THE LINE. IF THE LINE IS SUPER OR SUB, THEN	/A222
	JMP	DPLI11	/FIX IT. THIS IS OK GO DO THE CR		/A222
	SPA CLA		/THE LINE IS NG. GO UP OR DOWN PRIOR TO CR.	/A222
	JMP	DPLI12	/IS DOWN, GO UP.				/A222
	JMS	MOVEDN	/IS UP, MOVE DOWN				/A222
	JMP	DPLI11	/EXIT.						/A222
DPLI12,	JMS	MOVEUP	/IS DOWN, MOVE UP.				/A222

/WE ARE THROUGH WITH THIS PASS. IF THE NEXT PASS PLAG WAS INCREMENTED AT
/LEAST ONCE, DO ANOTHER PASS.

DPLI11,	JMS	SKNHST	/SKIP IF NOT HOST				/A234
	JMP	PRGSL1	/ENTER SPACING ROUTINE				/A234

	TAD	(CR)	/SEND A CARRIAGE RETURN TO END THE LINE
	JMS	PRCHAR
	ISZ	LPUPAS	/ Another pass completed.			/A092
	TAD	LPPASS	/DO WE NEED ANOTHER PASS?
	SNA CLA
	JMP	PRGSL1	/NO--ENTER THE SPACING ROUTINE. IT IS END OF PRINT LINE.
	JMP	DPLIN2	/YES--CONTINUE THE LOOP

/---------------
PAGE


OVTYPE=OLA
OVNUM=OVNUM+1
OVADR=OVADR+400
OVRLCA=PROVA+OVTYPE
DL=DLOPRO+OVNUM		/DISK BLOCK OF THIS OVERLAY
FIELD OVNUM%20+3

RELOC
*OVADR
RELOC OVRLCA

	-OVNUM
OVLN3A=.-OVRLCA
OBLN3A=OVTYPE+OVNUM

/LN03 OVERLAY A								/A247
/THIS OVERLAY IS USED FOR PRINTERS WHICH CAN DO EVERYTHING IN ONE PASS
/THEY MUST HONOR PLU,PLD,BS, AND SGR VALUES 0,1,4
/THEY SHOULD BE CAPABLE OF 7 BIT CHARACTER SELECTION AND BE CAPABLE
/OF PRINTING TECHNICAL CHARACTERS

/THIS OVERLAY IS RESPONSIBLE FOR JUSTIFYING (IF NECESSARY) AND PRINTING
/THE LINE OF TEXT WHICH HAS BEEN ASSEMBLED IN PRLBUF BY PRGTLN
/EXTERNAL ARGUMENT USED BY JUSTIFICATION OVERLAYS:

/PLPTR - A POINTER INTO THE LINE BUFFER.  INITIALLY, IT POINTS AT THE LAST
/         CHARACTER IN THE LINE, WHICH IS THE RETURN.

/PRLBUF - A LITERAL WHICH IS THE ADDRESS OF THE BEGINNING OF THE BUFFER; IT
/         IS THE ADDRESS AT WHICH THE FIRST CHARACTER IS STORED.

/PRQPIT - THE PITCH VALUE.  IT IS ZERO IF PROPORTIONAL PITCH IS SPECIFIED,
/         NON-ZERO OTHERWISE.

/PRRM   - THE JUSTIFICATION FLAG.  IT IS ZERO IF THE LINE IS NOT TO BE
/         JUSTIFIED; OTHERWISE, IT THE LENGTH OF THE LINE IN PRINT UNITS.

/PLNRCS - CHARACTER SIZE.  IT IS THE SIZE OF A CHARACTER IN PRINT UNITS.

/PRCCH  - PRINT HEAD POSITION.  INITIALLY, IT IS THE POSITION THE PRINT
/         HEAD IS IN.
/SOME DEFINITIONS WILL MAKE THE COMMENTS EASIER TO FOLLOW:

/CHARACTER - ONE OF THE ASCII VALUES WHICH FILL THE LINE BUFFER.  THE
/     POSSIBLE CHARACTERS ARE BACK SPACE, TAB, SPACE, AND TEXT.  THE
/     LAST POSITION IN THE LINE BUFFER IS FILLED BY A RETURN, WHICH IS
/     NOT A CHARACTER.

/DEAD KEY SEQUENCE - A SEQUENCE OF TEXT DELIMITED BY BACK SPACES
/     (I.E., TEXT1 BS TEXT2 BS TEXT3).  SUCH SEQUENCES ARE PRINTED WITH
/     THE CHARACTERS SUPERIMPOSED.

/POSITION - ALL THE CHARACTERS WHICH MAP TO THE SAME PHYSICAL PLACE ON
/     THE PRINTED PAGE ARE SAID TO BE THE SAME POSITION.  THEREFORE,
/     ALL THE CHARACTERS IN A DEAD KEY SEQUENCE ARE A POSITION.  A CHARACTER
/     WHICH IS NOT PART OF A DEAD KEY SEQUENCE IS A POSITION.  IN THIS
/     CODE, WE JUSTIFY BY POSITIONS; WE FIND THE WIDTH OF POSITIONS.  IT
/     IS AN IMPORTANT CONCEPT.

/WIDTH - THE SPACE OCCUPIED BY A POSITION WHEN IT IS PRINTED.  IN
/     NON-PROPORTIONAL PRINTING, THE WIDTH IS STANDARD (PLNRCS).  IN
/     PROPORTIONAL PRINTING, THE WIDTH VARIES DEPENDING ON THE CHARACTERS
/     IN THE POSITION.

/TEXT - ANY PRINTABLE ASCII CHARACTER


/WE ALSO MAKE SOME ASSUMPTIONS:

/1.  THE ONLY CHARACTERS IN THE BUFFER ARE RETURN, BACK SPACE, TAB, SPACE,
/    AND TEXT.

/2.  THE ONLY PLACE BACK SPACES OCCUR ARE BETWEEN TEXT.

/PLPTR currently addresses the return word in the buffer.  Bit 1 of this word
/is clear for a hard return and is set otherwise.  We save the type of the
/return for later use.

	AC2000			/ MASK SOFT RETURN BIT
	AND I	PLPTR
	DCA	PRJRET		/ RECORD TYPE OF RETURN
				/ PRJRET=2000 IF SOFT,
				/ PRJRET=0000 IF HARD, CENTERING, OR PARA MARKER

	DCA	PLPFLG		/ PROPORTIONAL PRINTING ON--OVLN3A	/C268
	JMS	PROV		/ CALL ROUTINE TO SEND DECPSP		/A268
		OBPLIN		/ BY LOADING THE "C" TYPE OVERLAY	/A268
	JMS	PLINIT		/ AND THEN CALLING THE ROUTINE		/A268

/We now set up some variables in order to process the line.  We want the
/following conditions to hold:

/PLPTR - Points to the return character (this already holds)

/PLCNT - Is the negative of the number of characters, including the return,
/		in the line.

/PLDIR - Is minus one so that processing can proceed from right to left.

LN3A0A,								/A211
	TAD	PLPTR	/Subtract the address of the return, and one more
	CMA
	TAD   (PRLBUF)	/From the address of the first character
	DCA	PLCNT	/And save as the buffer count

	AC7777
	DCA	PLDIR	/Set the direction of printing


/If the line ends with a soft return, we now strip all trailing spaces, tabs,
/and backspaces from the line.  If the line ends with a hard return, we do
/not strip these characters.  We strip in case of soft return because the
/trailing spaces interfere with justification, and should not appear in case
/the line is underlined.  In either case, when the loop is over, the following
/conditions will hold:

/1.  PLPTR will point to the right most character of the line which should
/	be printed (which will be a text character in case of a soft return
/	and the one before the return for a hard return).

/2.  PLCNT will be the negative of the number of characters from the beginning
/	of the buffer to the character pointed to by PLPTR.

LN3A0,
	JMS	PLNXT	/Look at the next character
	JMP	PRGSL1	/No more characters so the line was all blank--no
			/need to bother printing the line

	TAD	PRJRET		/ WAS IT A SOFT RETURN?
	SNA CLA
	JMP	LN3A1		/ NO, NO NEED TO STRIP TRAILING SPACES

	TAD I	PLPTR	/Get the character
	AND	P177	/Strip the mode bits
	TAD	(-SPC)	/Is it a space/tab/backspace?
	SPA SNA CLA	/skip if positive AND non-zero
	JMP	LN3A0	/Yes--continue stripping the line


/The line was not blank and must be printed.  We now initialize some variables
/used to gather information about the line for printing and justification.

LN3A1,
	TAD	PLCNT
	DCA	PRLBCT	/Save the stripped size of the line

	DCA	PLPS	/Clear position justification spacing
	DCA	PLSS	/Clear space justification spacing

	AC7777
	DCA	PRJPOS	/Initialize number of inter-position spaces
	DCA	PRJSPC	/Clear number of spaces

	DCA	PRRCH	/Clear right end of line position
	DCA	PRPASS	/Clear the pass counter

	DCA	PRJDEF	/Clear deficiency (no justification required yet)/A080
/This loop gathers information about the spacing and composition of the
/line by processing the characters from left to right.  When the loop
/completes, we will know:

/PRRCH - The offset of the right end of the line from the left margin in
/		elementary print units.

/PRJPOS - The number of inter-position spaces in the justifiable portion
/		of the line.

/PRJSPC - The number of spaces in the justifiable portion of the line.

/The loop also marks each character in the justifiable portion of the line
/by setting bit 0 of the character.  The justifiable portion of the line is
/that part which follows the rightmost tab space.

	AC4000		/We first set the count enable flag.  This flag is
	DCA	PRJENB	/to mark justifiable characters and is examined when
			/deciding whether to count them.  It is ultimately
			/cleared when the first tab is encountered.

/At the beginning of each iteration, PLPTR points to the first character in a
/position.  Each iteration treats the entire position with all of its dead
/keys, if any.

LN3A2,
	TAD I	PLPTR	/Get the character
	AND	P177	/Strip the mode bits

	TAD	(-SPC)	/Is it a space?
	SZA CLA
	JMP	LN3A3	/No, a text--jump to treat it

/The character is a space.  If it is part of a tab, the enable flag must be
/cleared.  If the enable flag has not been cleared by this time, we increment
/the number of justifiable spaces and fall through to the text counter.

	TAD I	PLPTR	/Retrieve the space character
	CMA RAL		/AC0=0 if the space is part of a tab, 1 otherwise
	AND	PRJENB	/AC=4000 if counting should still be enabled

	SZA		/Do not count the space if we are disabled
	ISZ	PRJSPC	/Increment the space count if we are enabled
	DCA	PRJENB	/And save the new enable flag
/This section handles characters.  If the enable flag is set, we count the
/position.

LN3A3,
	TAD	PRJENB	/Get the enable flag
	SZA CLA		/If the flag is clear, don't count the position

	ISZ	PRJPOS	/Increment the number of inter-position spaces
	NOP		/In case it skips (since it is initialized to -1)


/ NOW GET THE WIDTH OF THE POSITION.  IN THE CASE OF THE DP OR HOST
/ WE CAN SIMPLY GET THE VALUE PLNRCS.  IF AND WHEN MATRIX PRINTERS
/ CAN DO PROPORTIONAL PRINTING WE WILL HAVE TO DO SOMETHING DIFFERENT,
/ POSSIBLY JMS PLSPCX AS IS DONE FOR LQPS

	TAD	PLNRCS	/GET POSITION WIDTH				/C211

	TAD	PRRCH	/Update the right end measure
	DCA	PRRCH

/We now enter a sub-loop which moves the buffer pointer to the first
/character of the next position.  It also marks each non-backspace character
/as justifiable or not with the enable flag.  When we reach the next position,
/we continue the main loop.  If we just treated the last position, we leave
/the main loop and continue with processing.

LN3A4,
	TAD I	PLPTR	/Get the character
	TAD	PRJENB	/Mark it with the enable flag
	DCA I	PLPTR	/Save the character back into the buffer

LN3A4A,	JMS	PLCHBS	/Are there more characters?
	JMP	LN3A5	/No--leave the loop
	JMP	LN3A4A	/YES, A multinational PRECEDENCE		/A271
	JMP	LN3A4A	/YES, A TECHNICAL PRECEDENCE			/A233
	JMP	LN3A4	/Yes, in this position--continue the inner loop
	JMP	LN3A2	/Yes, in a new position--continue the outer loop

LN3A5,		/THE LINE HAS BEEN COMPLETELY MEASURED

/THIS SECTION PRINTS LINES TO A DRAFT PRINTER.
/MULTIPLE HITS AND UNDERLINING ARE HANDLED
/BY MAKING MULTIPLE PASSES THROUGH THE LINE.

/First, we determine whether or not justification is needed.

	TAD	PRBIF		/ GET BOLD INTERPRETATION FLAG		/A152
	SZA CLA								/A152
	AC2000			/ MASK AC1				/A152
	CMA			/ EITHER CLEAR IT OR LEAVE ALONE	/A152
	DCA	PRBIFM		/ SAVE AS BIT MASK			/A152

	DCA	PLSSC	/Clear the justification remainder
	DCA	LPUPAS	/Init the pass counter (for underlines).	/A092

	TAD	PRJRET		/ WAS IT A SOFT RETURN?
	SNA CLA
	JMP	LNLIN0		/ NO, NO JUSTIFICATION NEED BE DONE

	TAD	PRJSPC	/Are there any justifiable spaces?
	SNA CLA
	JMP	LNLIN0	/No--no justification can be done

	TAD	PRRM	/Does the ruler specify justification?
	SNA
	JMP	LNLIN0	/No--no need to do justification

/The AC now contains the desired justified line length.  We subtract that
/from the current line length to obtain the amount of deficiency.

	CIA
	TAD	PRRCH	/Subtract from  the current line length	
	SMA
	JMP	LNLIN0	/Line is already long enough or too long
	DCA	PRJDEF	/Save the negative of the deficiency

/We now divide the deficiency among the spaces.  The remainder is the negative
/of the number of additional space units which must be inerted, minus one.

	TAD	PRJSPC	/Divide the deficiency between the spaces
	JMS	PRDVID
	DCA	PLSS	/Save as the increased spacing to do

	TAD	PRJDEF	/Get the remainder
	DCA	PLSSC	/Save as the correction factor


LNLIN0,	CLA
	TAD	PRMCLM		/GET CURRENT MULTICOLUMN MARGIN
	JMS	LPIND		/INDENT THE MARGIN

	DCA	TCHBLD		/CLEAR TECH BOLD FLAG
	TAD	PRLBCT	/RESTORE THE CHARACTER COUNT FOR THE LINE
	DCA	PLCNT

	TAD   (PRLBUF)	/GET THE ADDRESS OF THE FIRST CHARACTER
	DCA	PLPTR	/INITIALIZE THE BUFFER POINTER

	AC0001
	DCA	PLDIR	/SET THE DIRECTION OF BUFFER SCAN

/FOR THE LN03 WE WILL MAKE ONLY ONE PASS THROUGH THE LINE
/WE WILL USE PLU AND PLD TO DO SUPERSCRIPTS AND SUBSCRIPTS
/WE WILL USE SGR TO ENABLE BOLD AND UNDERLINE
/WE WILL USE OUR NORMAL TECHNICAL CHARACTER SET PROCESSING
/ALGORITHMS AFTER WE HAVE TAKEN CARE OF THE PRINTING MODE,
/BUT BEFORE PRINTING THE CHARACTER.

/THE LN03 IS NOT CAPABLE OF EXTRA DARK PRINTING



	TAD	(OVLN3B
	JMS	PROV
		OBLN3B


/THE FOLLOWING DPLN JUSTIFICATION VARIABLES MUST APPEAR IN THE FIRST	/A215
/OF THE DPLN JUSTIFICATION OVERLAYS TO AVOID FORWARD REFERENCES TO	/A215
/OVERLAY VARIABLES WHICH ARE NOT LOADED YET ...				/A215

*LPSS		/FORCE A TEMPLATE MATCH					/A247

LPSS,	0	/Saves the value of PLSS between passes
LPSSC,	0	/Saves the value of PLSSC  between passes
PLSSC,	0	/Remainder
LPPASS,	0	/After a pass, is non-zero if another pass is needed
LPUPAS,	0	/Pass # (used to count how many times to bold the undrlns)/A092
PRBIFM,	0	/ MASK FOR #DARKNESS PASSES IF PRBIF>0			/A152
SISOFG,	0	/SHIFT IN SHIFT OUT FLAG. SET WHEN NEEDED FOR CODE 23
CHGSET,	0	/FLAG FOR CHANGING TO ORIGINAL G SET AFTER PRINTING
TCHBLD,	0	/FLAG FOR TECH CHARACTERS AND ELIMINATING BOLD INTERPRETATION.
CURPOS,	0	/CURRENT POSITION. +1 UP 1/2; 0 IS ON LINE;-1 IS	/A222
		/DOWN A HALF OF A LINE					/A222
PPUNDL,	0	/UNDERLINE PRINTED FLAG
LBADR,	"B&177+4000
/---------------
	PAGE

/THE MODE BITS OF EACH CHARACTER ARE NOT REARRANGED IN THIS OVERLAY
/THEREFORE, THEY ARE AS RECEIVED:

/AC  2  3			AC  4
/    0  0   NORMAL		    0     NOT BOLD
/    0  1   UNDERSCORE		    1     BOLD
/    1  0   SUPERSCRIPTED
/    1  1   SUBSCRIPT


/AC  0
/    0   THIS CHARACTER IS NOT A SPACE
/    1   THIS CHARACTER IS A REAL SPACE THAT IS JUSTIFIABLE
/------------------------
	PAGE

OVTYPE=OLB
OVNUM=OVNUM+1
OVADR=OVADR+400
OVRLCA=PROVA+OVTYPE
DL=DLOPRO+OVNUM		/DISK BLOCK OF THIS OVERLAY
FIELD OVNUM%20+3

RELOC
*OVADR
RELOC OVRLCA

	-OVNUM
OVLN3B=.-OVRLCA
OBLN3B=OVTYPE+OVNUM


LNLIN3,	TAD I	PLPTR		/GET THE FIRST CHARACTER IN ITS POSITION
	JMS	PRMODE		/PROCESS THE MODE BITS
				/...SUPER/SUB, BOLD, UNDERLINE


/NOW CHECK FOR THE CODE 21, 22, OR 23. THIS IS THE SEQUENCE FOR
/PRINTING A TECHNICAL CHARACTER IN ANOTHER CHARACTER SET. IT ALSO CHANGES TO
/G2 OR G3. THE CHARACTER STRING WILL BE TWO CHARACTERS. THE FIRST WILL BE THE
/CONTROL CHARACTER (21,22,OR 23), THE NEXT WILL BE THE CHARACTER OR THE "G"
/SET DESIRED. NOTE THAT THIS TWO CHARACTER STRING IS NOT CALLED A "G" STRING
/BUT IT COULD BE IF YOU SO DESIRE. THE CHARACTER ATTRIBUTES WILL ALSO BE
/ON THE CONTROL CHARACTER.

LNLIN4,	TAD I	PLPTR	/NOW CHECK TO SEE IF THE CHARACTER IS A CONTROL
	AND	P177	/CHARACTER THAT PRECEDES THE TECHNICAL CHARACTER.
	TAD	(-23)
	SZA		/23?
	IAC
	SNA		/OR 22?
	JMP	LNLI4G	/YES, PROCESS AND DON'T TRY TO JUSTIFY
	IAC
	SZA CLA		/IS IT 21?
	JMP	LNLI5B	/NO, GO PROCESS NORMALLY


/HERE FOR A CONTROL CHARACTER 21.   CHECK WHICH G SET IS LOADED IN G1	/A247
/IF LOCATION PCHBFO CONTAINS EITHER AN ASCII "A" OR "B", THEN PRINT
/SO,CHR,SI. IF PCHBFO DOES NOT CONTAIN EITHER "A" OR "B", THEN CHANGE TO
/B SET, PRINT SO,CHR,SI, AND RETURN TO THE OLD CHARACTER SET.


LNLI4E,	TAD	PCHFBO	/ SEE IF CHARACTER SET CHANGE IS NEEDED.
	TAD	(-"B+200) /IF CHARACTER IS B OR A, THEN NO CHANGE NEEDED.
	SZA
	IAC
	SNA CLA
	JMP	LNLI4F	/IS A. OR B. AS ABOVE

	AC7777		/SET FLAG TO INDICATE THAT WE HAVE CHANGED.
	DCA	CHGSET
	TAD	(LBADR)	/HAVE TO CHANGE.
	JMS	PRESCS
		PCSTG1
LNLI4F,	AC7777
	DCA	SISOFG	/SET FLAG TO SPAN CHARACTER WITH SO,CHR,SI
	JMP	LNLIN8	/EXIT AND DECREMENT THE HIT BITS

/HERE IF CODE 22 OR 23

LNLI4G,	TAD I	PLPTR	/ SEE IF CHARACTER SET CHANGE IS NEEDED.
	AND	P177	/MASK OFF MODE BITS
	TAD	(XSSS2-22)
	JMS	PRESCS
		PCSS	/CHANGE TO G2 OR G3
	CLA
	JMP	LNLIN8	/EXIT AND DECREMENT THE HIT BITS

/ DO BOLD INTERPRETATION STUFF HERE WHEN WE GET THE
/ REST OF THE STUFF WORKING

LNLI5B,	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET THE CHR BACK

/WE TEST TO SEE IF THE CODE IS THAT OF THE REQUIRED SPACE RQSP		/A216
/IF SO, WE PRINT THE SPACE CODE (40) INSTEAD OF THE REQUIRED SPACE (177)/A216
/CODE. THIS IS BECAUSE THE REQUIRED SPACE CODE IS JUST A PHONY CODE TO	/A216
/BE REPLACED AT THIS TIME SO THAT IT CANNOT CONTRIBUTE TO JUSTIFICATION	/A216

	AND	P177	/BIT 0 MAY OR MAY NOT BE SET
	TAD	(-RQSP)	/SUBTRACT REQUIRED SPACE CODE (177)		/A216
	SZA CLA		/SKIP IF REQUIRED SPACE				/A216
	JMP	LNLI5C	/NOT A REQUIRED SPACE
	TAD	(SPC)	/IT IS A REQUIRED SPACE.
	JMP	LNLI5E	/PRINT AN ASCII 40 (SPACE).

LNLI5C,			/SEE IF PRINT SHIFT OUT FOR A TECH CHR
	TAD	SISOFG		/IS SO,CHR,SI FLAG SET?
	SNA CLA
	JMP	LNLI5D		/NO FLAG, JUMP AHEAD
	TAD	(SO)		/GET SHIFT OUT
	JMS	PRCHAR

LNLI5D,	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET THE CHARACTER AGAIN
	AND	(200)	/SEE IF BOLD BIT IS SET
	R3L		/PUT IN POSITION FOR OTHER SCHEME
	DCA	PRBIC	/SAVE AS BOLD INTERPRETATION VALUE OF THIS CHR
	TAD	PRBIF	/SEE IF WE ARE DOING BOLD INTERPRETATION
	SZA CLA
	JMS	LPBI	/DO BOLD INTERPRETATION

	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET THE CHARACTER AGAIN--
LNLI5E,	AND	P177		/ strip unwanted bits
	JMS	PRCHAR		/PRINT IT AFTER ALL THE FINAGLEING.
	TAD	SISOFG		/DID WE PRINT SO?
	SZA CLA
	TAD	(SI)		/GET SHIFT IN.
	JMS	PRCHAR
	DCA	SISOFG		/CLEAR THE FLAG THAT GOT US HERE.
	TAD	CHGSET		/SEE IF WE MUST RESTORE THE OLD G SET.
	SNA CLA
	JMP	LNLI5F		/NO

	DCA	CHGSET		/YES, CLEAR FLAG
	TAD	(PCHFBO)	/RESTORE THE G SET BEING USED FOR BOLD
	JMS	PRESCS
		PCSTG1
	CLA
LNLI5F,

/NOW WE SEE IF ANY JUSTIFICATION IS NECESSARY.  ALL SPACES IN THE
/JUSTIFIABLE PORTION OF THE LINE ARE MARKED WITH BIT 0 SET.  AN EXTRA
/PLSS UNITS ARE ADDED TO EACH SUCH SPACE.  IN ADDITION, PLSSC IS
/INCREMENTED EACH TIME.  AS LONG AS IT REMAINS NEGATIVE OR ZERO, AN
/EXTRA UNIT IS ADDED TO EACH SPACE.  SINCE ONLY UNITS IN MULTIPLES OF
/SPACES ARE OUTPUT IN LPMOVE, LEFTOVER UNITS ARE RETAINED UNTIL THE
/NEXT SPACE.
	
/FIRST DETERMINE IF THE CHARACTER JUST OUTPUT WAS A JUSTIFIABLE SPACE.

	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET THE CHARACTER
	AND	(4177	/MASK LEAVING ONLY JUSTIFICATION BIT AND CHR
	TAD	(-4040)	/SEE IF WAS A JUSTIFIABLE SPACE
	SZA CLA		/SKIP IF SO
	JMP	LNLIN8	/IT WASN'T--NO JUSTIFICATION

	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET THE JUSTIFIABLE SPACE CODE
	MQL		/AND PUT IN MQ FOR LPMOVE

/Next determine how much space to add.  We increment PLSSC each time.  As soon
/as it reaches zero, we now longer use one additional unit.

	ISZ	PLSSC	/Increment the remainder counter
	NOP		/In case it skips

	TAD	PLSSC	/See if the remainder counter is still negative
	SPA CLA
	AC0001		/Yes--use one additional unit

	TAD	PLSS	/Recover the extra space sizing

/WE NOW HAVE THE AMOUNT OF EXTRA SPACING REQUIRED IN THE AC.  LPMOVE PADS
/OUT AS MUCH OF THE EXTRA SPACE AS IT CAN BY PRINTING THE CHARACTER IN THE
/MQ.

	JMS	LPMOVE	/DO THE MOTION

LNLIN8,	JMS	PLNXT	/POINT AT THE NEXT CHARACTER
	JMP	LNLI11	/NO MORE CHARACTERS--END OF THE LINE
	TAD I	PLPTR	/GET THE NEXT CHARACTER
	TAD	(-BS)	/IS IT A BACKSPACE?
	SZA CLA
	JMP	LNLIN3	/NO--THIS IS A DESIRED CHARACTER

/WE HAVE FOUND A BACKSPACE; OUTPUT IT & DO NEXT CHR.

	TAD I	PLPTR
	JMS	PRCHAR
	JMP	LNLIN8


/WE ARE THROUGH WITH THE LINE.  WE MUST MAKE SURE THE
/CARRIAGE IS RETURNED TO THE PROPER VERICAL POSITION
/AND THAT BOLDING/UNDERLINING ARE OFF

LNLI11,	JMS	PRMODE	/CALL ROUTINE TO CLEAR SUPER/SUB UNDL/BOLD
	TAD	(CR)	/SEND A CARRIAGE RETURN TO END THE LINE
	JMS	PRCHAR
	JMP	PRGSL1	/ENTER THE SPACING ROUTINE. IT IS END OF PRINT LINE.
/----------------
	PAGE

/THIS ROUTINE IS USED TO SEND OUT THE ESCAPE SEQUENCES TO SET UP THE
/PROPER VERTICAL POSITION, BOLD ATTRIBUTE, AND UNDERLINE ATTRIBUTE.
/IT USES ANSI SEQUENCES TO DO THIS (PLU, PLD AND SGR).  PRINTERS WHICH
/CAN ACCEPT THESE SEQUENCES DO NOT NEED A MULTIPLE PASS STRATEGY TO PRINT
/THE LINE.

/ORIGINALLY ADDED FOR THE "LN03 DRIVER"                         /A247

/IT HAS THE SAME NAME AS PRMODE SO IT CAN USE THE SAME VERT ROUTINE

/NOW THAT WE ARE SUPPORTING A PRINTER THAT HAS MULTIPLE FONT CAPABILITY,
/WE FIND THAT THE OLDS WAYS ARE NOT ADEQUATE.  PLU AND PLD ARE DEFINED
/TO BE HALF A LINE UP OR DOWN BASED ON THE CURRENT VERTICAL SPACING,
/WHICH IS CALCULATED FROM THE FONT.  THUS IF WE CHANGE FONTS IN THE
/MIDDLE OF A LINE USING BOLD INTERPRETATION, AND THE NEW FONT HAS A
/DIFFERENT SIZE THAN THE OLD FONT, WE MUST BE CAREFUL ABOUT THE ORDER
/IN WHICH WE DO THINGS, ELSE WE MAY INDUCE A BASELINE SHIFT WHEN GOING
/FROM BOLDED SUPERS TO NON BOLDED NORMAL TEXT OR VICE VERSA.

/THUS WE ESTABLISH THE FOLLOWING RULE:
/  1.	WHEN GOING FROM A NONBOLDED BASELINE TO A BOLDED SUPER OR SUB,
/	DO THE FONT CHANGE AFTER THE VERTICAL MOTION.
/  2.	WHEN GOING FROM A BOLDED SUPER OR SUB TO A NONBOLDED CHR
/	IN A DIFFERENT VERTICAL POSITION, DO THE FONT CHANGE BEFORE
/	THE VERTICAL MOTION

PRMODE, XX
	DCA	PRMTMP		/SAVE ARGUMENT FOR LATER USE
	TAD	PRMTMP
        BSW
        AND     (0014
        TAD     (LNVECT         / ADD ADDRESS OF THE MATRIX
        DCA     PRJUMP          / SAVE TO INDIRECT JUMP THROUGH
	JMP	PRMOD1		/ TO PRESERVE TEMPLATE MATCH

PRJUMP, 0
NEWPOS, 0                       / NEW VERTICAL SELECT
OLDPOS, 0                       / OLD VERTICAL SELECT
ULBREQ, 0                       / UNDERLINE/BOLD STATUS REQUEST
PRMTMP,	0			/ TEMPORARY STORAGE OF ARGUMENT

PRMOD1,	JMS	PROV		/ LOAD OVERLAY CONTAINING VERT
		OBVERT		/
        JMP I   PRJUMP          / EXECUTE THE APPROPRIATE ROUTINE

LNSUND, JMS     VERT            / SELECT NORMAL VERTICAL POSITION
        AC0004                  / REQUEST UNDERLINE
        JMP     LNRSTU          / GO SET INTO REQUEST WORD

LNVECT,		/FROM NORMAL;SUPER;SUB
	JMP	LNRMAL; 000; 001; 101	/REQUEST NORMAL
	JMP	LNSUND; 000; 001; 101	/REQUEST UNDERLINE
        JMP     LSUPER; 101; 000; 102	/REQUEST SUPER
	JMP     LSUBSC; 001; 002; 000	/REQUEST SUBSCT

LSUBSC,	IAC                     / NEWPOS WILL BE SELECT 2
LSUPER,	IAC                     / NEWPOS WILL BE SELECT 1
LNRMAL, JMS     VERT            / SELECT 0 VERTICAL

/NOW HANDLE UNDERLINE ATTRIBUTE
LNRSTU,	DCA     ULBREQ          / CLEAR UNDERLINE/BOLD REQUEST
	TAD	PRMTMP		/ GET THE CHARACTER BACK
        AND     (200)           / ISOLATE THE BOLD BIT
        BSW                     / GET INTO
        CLL RAR                 / BIT 11 (WEIGHT 1)
        TAD     ULBREQ          / SET UP
        DCA     ULBREQ          / NEW UNDERLINE/BOLD REQUEST
        TAD     ULBREQ          / SEE IF SAME STATUS AS BEFORE
        CIA                     /
        TAD     UNDERL          /
        SNA CLA                 /
        JMP I   PRMODE          / YES, NO FURTHER ACTION NECESSARY

/WE HAVE DETECTED THE NEED FOR A CHANGE IN THE BOLD AND/OR UNDERLINE
/ATTRIBUTE.  WE WILL ISSUE SGR FIRST WITH A ZERO PARAMETER, AND
/THEN WITH THE BOLD NUMERIC VALUE (1) IF THAT IS WHAT IS REQUIRED,
/AND THEN WITH THE UNDERLINE NUMERIC VALUE (4), IF THAT IS WHAT IS
/REQUIRED

        JMS     PRESCS          / CALL SGR SEQUENCE
                PCSGR           / RESET WITH CLEAR AC

	TAD	PRBIF		/ SEE IF DOING BOLD INTERPRETATION
	CMA			/
        AND     ULBREQ          / AND WITH REQUESTED VALUE
	AND	(0001)		/ LOOK ONLY AT BOLD BIT
        SZA                     / SKIP AND DO A HARMLESS AND OR
        JMS     PRESCS          / SET THE BOLD ATTRIBUTE
                PCSGR           / ANSI SGR SEQUENCE

        AC0004                  / SET THE UNDERLINE MASK
        AND     ULBREQ          / AND WITH REQUESTED VALUE
        SZA                     / SKIP AND DO A HARMLESS AND OR
        JMS     PRESCS          / SET THE UNDERLINE ATTRIBUTE
                PCSGR           / ANSI SGR SEQUENCE
                CLA             / CLEAR RETURNED VALUE

        TAD     ULBREQ          / GET NEW VALUES FOR BOLD/UNDERLINE
        DCA     UNDERL          / SAVE FOR POSTERITY
	JMP I	PRMODE		/ AND EXIT
/--------------------
	PAGE


OVTYPE=OLA
OVNUM=OVNUM+1
OVADR=OVADR+400
OVRLCA=PROVA+OVTYPE
DL=DLOPRO+OVNUM		/DISK BLOCK OF THIS OVERLAY
FIELD OVNUM%20+3

RELOC
*OVADR
RELOC OVRLCA

	-OVNUM



/THE SAVE AND RESTORE ROUTINES ARE USED TO HANDLE HEADERS AND FOOTERS.  THE
/ESSENTIAL IDEA IS THAT WHEN A HEADER OR FOOTER PRINT CONTROL BLOCK IS
/ENCOUNTERED, THE STATE OF THE PRINTER IS RECORDED.  IT IS RECORDED IN
/SUCH A WAY THAT THAT STATE CAN BE RESTORED LATER AT ANY TIME.

/THEN, WHEN IT COMES TIME TO PRINT A HEADER/FOOTER, THE CURRENT STATE OF
/THE PRINTER IS SAVED, AND THE APPROPRIATE SAVED STATE IS RESTORED.  IN THIS
/STATE, THE PRINTER CAN PRINT THE HEADER/FOOTER.  WHEN THE HEADER/FOOTER
/COMPLETES, THE OLD CURRENT STATE IS RESTORED, AND PRINTING RESUMES WHERE
/IT LEFT OFF.

/THE STATE OF THE PRINTER CAN BE DIVIDED INTO TWO PARTS:

/1.  PRINTER VARIABLES
/2.  POSITION IN THE FILE

/THE PRINTER VARIABLES WHICH MUST BE SAVED DURING A STATE SAVE ARE:

/1.  PRRLSZ - THE LINE SIZE SETTING MANDATED BY THE RULER.  THIS IS SAVED
/             SO THAT THE RULER IN EFFECT AT THE TIME OF THE SAVE CAN TAKE
/             EFFECT AGAIN.

/2.  PRRM - THE JUSTIFICATION SETTING MANDATED BY THE RULER.  AGAIN, THIS
/           IS SAVED SO THAT THE RULER IN EFFECT AT THE TIME OF THE SAVE CAN
/           TAKE EFFECT AGAIN.

/3.  PRGCBK - THE BACKUP LOCATION FOR THE READ ROUTINE, PRGCHR.  THIS IS
/             SAVED SO THAT ANY BACKED UP READ IS STILL BACKED UP WHEN
/             THE STATE IS RESTORED.


/THE POSITION IN THE FILE MUST ALSO BE SAVED.  THE IDEA HERE IS THAT THE
/STATE OF THE FILE SYSTEM IS SAVED, SO THAT WE CAN LATER MOVE TO THE
/APPROPRIATE PART OF THE FILE WHEN IT IS TIME TO RESTORE.  THIS WORKS
/QUITE NICELY WHEN READING FROM A FILE, SINCE IT IS POSSIBLE TO BACK UP
/IN A FILE.  HOWEVER, WHEN READING CHARACTERS FROM THE EDITOR UNDER LIST
/PROCESSING, IT IS NOT POSSIBLE TO BACK UP.  THEREFORE, TWO STRATEGIES ARE
/EMPLOYED; ONE FOR FILE READING, ONE FOR LIST PROCESSING.

/1.  FILE SYSTEM SAVE/RESTORE IS HANDLED JOINTLY BY THE PRINTER AND THE FILE
/    SYSTEM.  FOUR VARIABLES WITHIN THE FILE SYSTEM DEFINE THE STATE OF THE
/    FILE READ; THESE VARIABLES ARE REMOTELY SAVED AND RESTORED BY THE
/    PRINTER.  WHEN RESTORING, TWO ROUTINES MUST BE INVOKED FOLLOWING THE
/    VARIABLE RESTORATION TO FILL THE BUFFERS BACK UP.  THE ADVANTAGE OF THIS
/    SCHEME IS THAT THERE IS NO RESTRICTION OF HEADER/FOOTER LENGTH.

/2.  LIST PROCESSING SAVE/RESTORE IS HANDLED SOLELY BY THE PRINTER.  WHEN
/    THE HEADER/FOOTER BLOCK IS ENCOUNTERED, IT IS READ CHARACTER BY
/    CHARACTER AND WRITTEN OUT TO A SPECIAL DISK BUFFER.  WHEN THE HEADER/
/    FOOTER IS RESTORED, THE CHARACTERS ARE READ BACK FROM THE DISK.  HENCE,
/    DURING LIST PROCESSING THE PRINTER MUST READ FROM THE EDITOR WHEN
/    PRINTING NORMAL TEXT, AND FROM THE DISK BUFFER WHEN PROCESSING A
/    HEADER/FOOTER.  UNDER THIS SCHEME THE LENGTH OF A HEADER OR FOOTER,
/    INCLUDING RULERS, IS LIMITED TO FIVE BLOCKS.


/THE ALERT READER MAY WONDER WHY THE TWO CASES ARE TREATED SEPARATELY.  A
/FIVE BLOCK RESTRICTION IS NOT SEVERE, SO WHY NOT BUFFER ALL HEADER/FOOTERS
/ON DISK?  WHEN DOING LIST PROCESSING, THE SCROLL BUFFERS ARE NOT IN USE,
/AND THESE ARE USED TO BUFFER HEADER/FOOTERS BEFORE WRITING TO DISK.  THERE
/IS NO SUITIABLE BUFFER TO USE IN THE GENERAL CASE.


/THIS ROUTINE SAVES THE STATE OF THE PRINTER AND FILE SYSTEM.  IT IS USED
/TO "REMEMBER" HEADERS AND FOOTERS SO THAT THEY CAN BE PRINTED LATER AND
/TO "REMEMBER" OUR PLACE WHEN WE PRINT HEADERS/FOOTERS SO THAT WE CAN
/PICK UP WHERE WE LEFT OFF LATER.

/THE ARGUMENT TO THIS ROUTINE IS ZERO IF THE STATE IS TO BE SAVED FOR
/RESTORATION AS THE CURRENT LOCATION; MINUS ONE IF THE STATE IS TO BE
/SAVED FOR RESTORATION AS A FOOTER; AND ONE IF THE STATE IS TO BE SAVED
/FOR RESTORATION AS A HEADER.

/AC=TYPE OF SAVE
/JMS	PRSAVE
/AC=0


	OBPRSV=OVTYPE+OVNUM						/A185
PRSAVE,	XX

	DCA	PRFLAG	/SAVE AS THE TYPE OF PRINTING IN PROGRESS

/UP TO EIGHT WORDS MAY BE SAVED WHEN SAVING THE STATE. HERE, WE COMPUTE (M034)
/THE OFFSET INTO THE SAVE TABLE CORRESPONDING TO THE REQUESTED TYPE OF
/SAVE.

	TAD	(10)	/MULTIPLY THE ARGUMENT BY 8(10)	/M034
	JMS	PRMULT
		PRFLAG

/THE FIRST SECTION OF THE SAVE TABLE CONTAINS THE FOOTER SAVE; THE SECOND
/SECTION CONTAINS THE CURRENT TEXT SAVE; AND THE THIRD SECTION CONTAINS
/THE HEADER SAVE.

	TAD (PRSAV+10-1) /ADD THE ADDRESS OF THE SAVE TABLE PLUS EIGHT /M034
	DCA	X0	/SAVE IN AN AUTO-INCREMENT REGISTER

/WE NOW SAVE THE FOUR CRUCIAL PRINTER VARIABLES IN THE SAVE TABLE. /M034

	TAD	PRRM	/SAVE THE JUSTIFICATION SETTING
	DCA I	X0

	TAD	PRRLSZ	/SAVE THE LINE SPACING
	DCA I	X0

	TAD	PRGCBK	/SAVE THE BACKUP LOCATION
	DCA I	X0
	
	TAD	RULRRM	/A034 SAVE THE RIGHT MARGIN
	DCA I	X0	/A034
	
	JMS	PRTRNC	/GET THE PROPER RULER SETTING FOR THIS SAVE

/NOW, OUR STATE SAVE METHOD DIVERGES, DEPENDING ON WHETHER WE ARE DOING
/LIST PROCESSING OR NOT.

	TAD	LPFLAG	/ARE WE DOING LIST PROCESSING?
	SZA CLA
	JMP	PRSAV2	/YES--JUMP TO HANDLE A LIST PROCESSING SAVE


/WE ARE DOING A REGULAR FILE SYSTEM SAVE.  WE SIMPLY SAVE THE FOUR VALUES
/FROM THE FILE SYSTEM AND SAVE THEM IN THE SAVE TABLE.

/GET THE ADDRESS PRECEEDING THE FIRST OF THE FOUR VARIABLES TO BE SAVED.
/THE FOUR VARIABLES ARE ALL IN A BLOCK.

	TAD   (FSSAV-1)	/GET ONE BEFORE THE BLOCK TO BE SAVED

	DCA	X1	/SAVE IN AN AUTO-INCREMENT REGISTER
	TAD	(-4)	/SET UP THE LOOP COUNT
	DCA	T1

/THIS LOOP SAVES THE FOUR FILE SYSTEM VARIABLES.

PRSAV1,	TAD I	X1	/GET A VARIABLE
	DCA I	X0	/SAVE THE VALUE
	ISZ	T1	/HAVE WE READ ALL FOUR?
	JMP	PRSAV1	/NO--CONTINUE THE LOOP

	JMP I	PRSAVE	/YES--RETURN



/THIS SECTION HANDLES STATE SAVES FOR LIST PROCESSING.  IF WE ARE NOT
/SAVING A HEADER/FOOTER, WE ARE DONE AND MAY RETURN IMMEDIATELY.

PRSAV2,
	TAD	PRFLAG	/WAS THIS A HEADER/FOOTER SAVE?
	SNA
	JMP I	PRSAVE	/NO--RETURN


/NOW WE WRITE THE HEADER/FOOTER TO THE APPROPRIATE DISK BUFFER.

	JMS	GBLOCK	/INITIALIZE THE FIRST BLOCK TO WRITE
	JMS	WBLOCK	/WRITE THE HEADER/FOOTER TO THE DISK BUFFER

/THE HEADER/FOOTER IS NOW RECORDED ON DISK.  THE HEADER/FOOTER CONTROL
/BLOCK CODE, WHICH REQUESTS HEADER/FOOTER SAVES, NOW NEEDS TO MAKE A PASS
/THROUGH THE HEADER/FOOTER TO RECORD SOME INFORMATION ABOUT IT.  WE NOW
/SET THINGS UP SO THAT SUBSEQUENT READS WILL COME FROM THE BUFFER READER
/FOR THE DURATION OF THE HEADER/FOOTER PROCESSING.

	TAD	PRFLAG	/RECOVER THE ARGUMENT
	JMS	INITRD	/INITIALIZE SO THAT THE BUFFER READER CAN BE USED

	JMP I	PRSAVE	/AND RETURN




/THIS ROUTINE RESTORES THE STATE OF THE PRINTER AND FILE SYSTEM.  IT IS USED
/TO "RECOVER" SAVED HEADERS AND FOOTERS SO THAT THEY CAN BE PRINTED AND
/TO "RECOVER" OUR PLACE AFTER WE PRINT HEADERS/FOOTERS SO THAT WE CAN
/PICK UP WHERE WE LEFT OFF.

/THE ARGUMENT TO THIS ROUTINE IS ZERO IF REGULAR TEXT IS TO BE RESTORED; MINUS
/ONE IF THE FOOTER IS TO BE RESTORED; AND ONE IF THE HEADER IS TO BE RESTORED.

/AC=TYPE OF SAVE
/JMS	PRREST
/AC=0

	OBREST=OVTYPE+OVNUM						/A185
PRREST,	XX

	DCA	PRFLAG	/SAVE THE TYPE OF RESTORATION

/WE COMPUTE THE OFFSET INTO THE SAVE TABLE OF THE VALUES WHICH MUST BE
/RESTORED.

	TAD	(10)	/MULTIPLY THE ARGUMENT BY EIGHT	(10)	/M034
	JMS	PRMULT
		PRFLAG

	TAD (PRSAV+10-1)/ADD THE ADDRESS OF SAVE TABLE TO GET THE OFFSET/M034
	DCA	X0	/SAVE IN AN AUTO-INCREMENT REGISTER

/NEXT, RESTORE THE THREE PRINTER VARIABLES.

	TAD I	X0	/RESTORE THE JUSTIFICATION VALUE
	DCA	PRRM

	TAD I	X0	/RESTORE THE LINE SPACING
	DCA	PRRLSZ

	TAD I	X0	/RESTORE THE BACKUP BUFFER
	DCA	PRGCBK
	TAD I 	X0	/RESTORE THE RIGHT MARGIN			/M034
	DCA RULRRM							/M034

/THE AMOUNT OF SPACING WHICH GOES BETWEEN LINES IS THE SUM OF PRRLSZ AND
/PRQLSZ, WITH THE EXCEPTION THAT PRQLSZ DOES NOT CONTRIBUTE WHEN PRINTING
/A HEADER OR FOOTER.  THIS ROUTINE COMPUTES THE LINE SPACING GIVEN THAT
/CONSTRAINT.

	JMS	PRTRNC	/RESTORE THE VALUE OF PRLLSZ

/THE REST OF THE RESTORATION PROCESS DIFFERS DEPENDING ON WHETHER WE ARE
/DOING LIST PROCESSING.

	TAD	LPFLAG	/ARE WE DOING LIST PROCESSING?
	SZA CLA
	JMP	PRRES2	/YES--JUMP TO HANDLE IT

/IF A HEADER OR FOOTER WAS JUST PRINTED, FINDING THE END OF IT CAUSED THE
/READ ROUTINE TO SET RDCHAR SO THAT ALL SUBSEQUENT READS WOULD RETURN ZERO.
/WE MUST RESET THE READ LINK SO THAT WE MAY CONTINUE READING.

	TAD   (RDFILE)	/GET THE FILE READING LABEL
	DCA	RDCHAR	/RESET THE READ LINK


/RESTORE THE FOUR SAVED VALUES TO THE FILE SYSTEM.

	TAD  (FSSAV-1)	/GET ONE BEFORE THE BLOCK TO RESTORE

	DCA	X1	/SAVE IN AN AUTO-INCREMENT REGISTER
	TAD	(-4)	/SET THE LOOP COUNT
	DCA	T1


/THIS LOOP RESTORES THE FOUR VALUES.

PRRES1,	TAD I	X0	/GET THE SAVED VALUE
	DCA I	X1	/RESTORE THE VALUE
	ISZ	T1	/HAVE WE RESTORED ALL FOUR?
	JMP	PRRES1	/NO--CONTINUE THE LOOP


/NOW, MAKE TWO CALLS TO THE FILE SYSTEM TO COMPLETE THE RESTORE.

	JMS	RDFTAB	/INITIALIZE THE TABLE OF BLOCK POINTERS

	JMS	RDFBUF	/READ IN THE NEXT BLOCK OF TEXT
	CLA		/AN ERROR SHOULD NOT OCCUR

	JMP I	PRREST	/AND RETURN


/THIS SECTION HANDLES COMPLETING LIST PROCESSING RESTORATION.

PRRES2,

/WE SET THE READ LINK TO THE EDITOR READ.  THIS WILL BE CHANGED BELOW IF
/WE ARE RESTORING A HEADER/FOOTER.

	TAD   (RDEDIT)	/SET THE READ ROUTINE TO EDITOR READ
	DCA	RDCHAR

	TAD	PRFLAG	/ARE WE DOING A REGULAR TEXT RESTORE?
	SNA
	JMP I	PRREST	/YES--RETURN IMMEIDATELY

/WE MUST READ SUBSEQUENT CHARACTERS FROM THE DISK BUFFER.  THIS ROUTINE
/SETS THINGS UP SO THAT THE READS CAN BE DONE.

	JMS	INITRD	/INITIALIZE FOR READING FROM THE DISK BUFFER
	JMP I	PRREST	/AND RETURN



/THIS ROUTINE INTIALIZES THE APPROPRIATE VARIABLES SO THAT ALL READS WHICH
/FOLLOW WILL COME FROM THE HEADER/FOOTER BUFFER.  THE ARGUMENT IN THE AC
/IS ONE IF A HEADER SHOULD BE READ, AND TWO IF A FOOTER SHOULD BE READ.

/AC=ARGUMENT
/JMS	INITRD
/AC=0


INITRD,
	XX

/GBLOCK WILL DETERMINE THE FIRST BLOCK NUMBER OF THE APPROPRIATE BUFFER.  THE
/ARGUMENT IT EXPECTS HAPPENS TO BE THE ARGUMENT THIS ROUTINE RECEIVES.

	JMS	GBLOCK	/SET THE FIRST BLOCK OF THE DISK BUFFER
	JMS	RBLOCK	/READ THE FIRST BLOCK FROM THE DISK INTO THE
			/RESIDENT BUFFER

	TAD   (RDBUFF)	/SET THE READ ROUTINE LINK
	DCA	RDCHAR

	JMP I	INITRD	/AND RETURN



/THIS ROUTINE READS THE NEXT BLOCK FROM THE DISK BUFFER AREA INTO THE
/RESIDENT BUFFER AREA AND INITIALIZES THE VARIABLES OF THE BUFFER
/READING ROUTINE SO THAT THE BLOCK MAY BE READ CHARACTER BY CHARACTER.

/AC=0
/JMS	RBLOCK
/AC=0

RBLOCK,
	XX

/FIRST, WE INITIALIZE THE VARIABLES USED BY THE READ BUFFER ROUTINE (RDBUFF).

	TAD (STXBUF-1)	/SET THE POINTER INTO THE RESIDENT BUFFER
	DCA	RDBPTR

	TAD	(-401)	/SET THE CHARACTER COUNT OF THE RESIDENT BUFFER
	DCA	RDCCNT

/NEXT, WE READ THE DESIRED BLOCK FROM THE DISK INTO THE RESIDENT BUFFER.

	TAD	RDBUF1		/SET THE DATA FIELD OF THE BUFFER
	DCA PRQB+RXQBFD

	TAD	RDBLCK		/SET THE BLOCK TO READ
	JMS	PRQRX		/READ THE BLOCK
		RXERD+4000	/READ					/A207
		0		/SYSTEM DISK				/A207
		STXBUF		/BUFFER ADDRESS				/A207
	JMP	PRQSDE		/IF ERROR GO REPORT IT			/A207

/NOW, WE RESTORE THE INFORMATION BLOCK.
	
	TAD   (CDFMYF)	/SET THE DATA FIELD BACK
	DCA PRQB+RXQBFD

/THE NEXT BLOCK READ FROM THE DISK BUFFER IS THE ONE FOLLOWING THE ONE
/JUST READ.

	ISZ	RDBLCK	/INCREMENT THE BLOCK FOR NEXT TIME
	
	JMP I	RBLOCK	/AND RETURN


/THIS OVERLAY ENTRY IS A LINK TO THE SUBROUTINE RBLOCK.

OVRDBL=.-OVRLCA
OBRDBL=OVTYPE+OVNUM

	JMS	RBLOCK	/READ THE NEXT BLOCK
	JMP	RDBUFF	/RETURN TO THE BUFFER READ ROUTINE


PAGE

/THIS ROUTINE SETS THE CURRENT VALUE OF VERTICAL SPACING (PRLLSZ)
/BY ADDING THE LINE SPACING AS DEFINED BY THE RULER (PRRLSZ)
/AND THE NUMBER OF EXTRA HALF LINES DEFINED IN THE PRINT MENU (PRQLSZ)
/IF PRINTING A HEADER OR FOOTER, PRQLSZ IS IGNORED

/MADE FUNCTIONAL FOR ALL TYPES OF PRINTERS				/A212

PRTRNC,	XX
	TAD	PRFLAG	/ARE WE PRINTING A HEADER/FOOTER?		
	SNA CLA								
	TAD	PRQLSZ	/ADD THE EXTRA LINE SPACING FROM THE PRINT MENU
			/ONLY IF PRINTING REGULAR TEXT			
	TAD	PRRLSZ	/ADD THE LINE SIZE DEFINED BY THE RULER

	DCA	PRLLSZ	/SAVE AS THE LINE SIZE
	JMP I	PRTRNC	/AND RETURN

/ THIS ROUTINE STRIPS CHARACTERS UNTIL A NEW LINE IS FOUND.  IT MAKES
/ A NORMAL RETURN IF THE EOC WAS FOUND, A SKIP RETURN IF EOL,
/ AND REPORTS MISMATCHED CONTROLS IF EOF IS FOUND			/A184

	OBHIGS=OVTYPE+OVNUM						/A185
PRHIGS,
	XX

PRHIG1,
	JMS	PTSTST		/ SEE IF USER WANTS TO STOP		/A182
	JMS	PRGCHC		/ GET A CHARACTER			/C192
	JMP I	PRHIGS		/ END OF CONTROL			/C192

	AND	P177		/ STRIP MODE BITS
	TAD	(-12)		/ CHECK FOR A NEW LINE
	SZA CLA
	JMP	PRHIG1		/ KEEP LOOKING
	ISZ	PRHIGS		/ SKIP RETURN FOR EOL
	JMP I	PRHIGS		/ NEW LINE FOUND--RETURN

/THIS ROUTINE RECEIVES MINUS ONE AS AN ARGUMENT IF A FOOTER IS TO BE READ
/FROM DISK, AND PLUS ONE IF A HEADER IS TO BE READ.  IT SETS THE LOCATION
/RDBLCK WITH THE BEGINNING DISK BLOCK OF THE IDSK BUFFER OR THE HEADER OR
/FOOTER.

/AC=ARGUMENT
/JMS	GBLOCK
/AC=0

GBLOCK,
	XX

	SPA CLA
	TAD (DLFDTR-DLHDTR)	/THE FIRST BLOCK OF THE FOOTER DISK BUFFER
	TAD   (DLHDTR)		/THE FIRST BLOCK OF THE HEADER DISK BUFFER
	DCA	RDBLCK	/SAVE THE FIRST BLOCK OF THE APPROPRIATE DISK BUFFER
	
	JMP I	GBLOCK	/AND RETURN


/THIS SECTION BUFFERS A LIST PROCESSING HEADER/FOOTER BEGINNING WITH THE
/BLOCK NUMBER IN RDBLCK.  IT WILL WRITE UP TO FIVE BLOCKS.  IF A
/HEADER/FOOTER EXCEEDS FIVE BLOCKS, THAT PART WHICH EXCEEDS IS LOST.

/REWRITTEN TO USE RDBUF IN THIS FIELD TO SAVE SPACE AND SIMPLIFY	/C190

/AC=0
/JMS	WBLOCK
/AC=0

WBLOCK,	XX

/ WE MUST SET UP SOME VARIABLES WHICH DRIVE THE LOOP WHICH FOLLOWS.

	TAD	(-5)	/SET THE MAXIMUM NUMBER OF BLOCKS WHICH MAY BE WRITTEN
	DCA	WBBCNT

/EACH ITERATION OF THIS LOOP SEES ONE BLOCK BEING WRITTEN.  IF THE CONTROL
/BLOCK ENDS IN THE MIDDLE OF A BLOCK, THAT BLOCK IS WRITTEN BEFORE RETURN.

WBLCK1,

/400 CHARACTERS MAY BE WRITTEN INTO A BLOCK.  THIS INNER LOOP WRITES
/CHARACTERS UNTIL THE BUFFER IS FULL OR AN END OF FILE/END OF COMMAND IS
/FOUND.

	TAD	(-401)	/SET THE MAXIMUM CHARACTER COUNT PLUS ONE
	DCA	WBCCNT

	TAD  (RDBUF-1)	/GET ONE BEFORE THE BEGINNING OF THE BUFFER
	DCA	WBBPTR	/SAVE AS THE BUFFER POINTER

/THIS LOOP INSERTS CHARACTERS INTO THE RESIDENT BUFFER UNTIL IT FILLS OR
/UNTIL THE END OF THE HEADER/FOOTER IS FOUND.

WBLCK2,
	ISZ	WBCCNT	/IS THERE ROOM IN THE BUFFER?
	JMP	WBLCK3	/YES--JUMP TO INSERT THE NEXT CHARACTER

	ISZ	WBBCNT	/IS THERE A BLOCK AFTER THIS ONE?
	JMP	WBLCK7	/YES--WRITE THE BUFFER AND TRY AGAIN

			/THERE IS NO MORE ROOM IN THE DISK BUFFER
			/WRITE AN EOC IN THE LAST LOCATION OF THE BUFFER/C190
			/AND GO STRIP THE REST OF THE BLOCK

	TAD	(EPC)		/ LOAD END OF CONTROL CODE		/A190
	DCA I	WBBPTR		/ STUFF IN END OF BUFFER		/A190

/THE FINAL BLOCK MUST BE WRITTEN TO DISK, BUT FIRST WE MUST STRIP
/THE REMAINING CHRACTERS IN THE HEADER FOOTER

WBLCK5,	JMS	READCH	/READ A CHARACTER--HAVE WE FOUND THE END YET?
	SZA CLA
	JMP	WBLCK5	/NO--CONTINUE STRIPPING CHARACTERS
	TAD	T1	/SEE IF EOF					/A190
	SNA CLA		/						/C241
	JMP	WBLCK8	/YES (CAREFUL--THIS IS LIST PROCESSING)		/A190
	JMP	WBLCK6	/GO WRITE THE BLOCK, ETC			/A190

WBLCK3,
	JMS	READCH	/GET THE NEXT CHARACTER TO WRITE IN THE BUFFER.
	ISZ	WBBPTR	/WRITE IN THE NEXT BUFFER LOCATION

/WE NOW HAVE A CHARACTER TO WRITE IN THE BUFFER--BE IT A NEW CHARACTER (IN
/WHICH CASE IT IS WRITTEN IN A NEW BUFFER LOCATION) OR AN END OF CONTROL
/GENERATED BECAUSE THE DISK BUFFER WAS FULL (IN WHICH CASE IT IS OVERWRITTEN
/IN THE LAST BUFFER LOCATION).

	SNA		/IF THE CHR RETURNED IS ZERO			/A190
	TAD	T1	/THEN WRITE T1 (EOC OR EOF)			/A190
	DCA I	WBBPTR	/WRITE THE CHARACTER IN THE RESIDENT BUFFER
	TAD I	WBBPTR	/RECOVER THE CHARACTER JUST WRITTEN
	SZA		/SEE IF END OF FILE				/A190
	TAD	(-EPC)	/OR END OF CONTROL				/A190

/THE AC NOW CONTAINS ZERO IF BUFFERING IS COMPLETE, AND NON-ZERO OTHERWISE.
/CONTINUE THE LOOP UNLESS BUFFERING IS COMPLETE.

	SZA CLA
	JMP	WBLCK2	/BUFFERING INCOMPLETE--CONTINUE THE LOOP


WBLCK6,	TAD	RDBLCK		/LOAD THE BLOCK NUMBER			/A207
	JMS	PRQRX		/WRITE THE BLOCK ONTO DISK
		RXEWT+4000	/WRITE					/A207
		0		/SYSTEM DRIVE				/A207
		RDBUF		/BUFFER ADDRESS				/A207
	JMP	PRQSDE		/IF ERROR GO REPORT IT			/A207

	
	JMP I	WBLOCK	/AND RETURN


/THE BUFFER IS FULL AND MUST BE WRITTEN TO DISK.

WBLCK7,
	TAD	RDBLCK		/LOAD THE BLOCK NUMBER			/A241
	JMS	PRQRX		/WRITE THE BLOCK
		RXEWT+4000	/WRITE					/A207
		0		/SYSTEM DRIVE				/A207
		RDBUF		/BUFFER ADDRESS				/A207
	JMP	PRQSDE		/IF ERROR GO REPORT IT			/A207
	ISZ	RDBLCK		/BUMP BLOCK NUMBER			/C207
	JMP	WBLCK1		/CONTINUE THE LOOP

WBLCK8,			/MISSING CONTROL IN LP HEADER/FOOTER		/A201
	JMP	MISCTL		/GO REPORT MISSING CONTROL		/A201

WBBPTR,	0	/A POINTER INTO THE RESIDENT BUFFER BEING WRITTEN
WBCCNT,	0	/A COUNT OF ROOM REMAINING IN THE RESIDENT BUFFER
WBBCNT,	0	/A COUNT OF BLOCKS REMAINING TO BE WRITTEN
/-------------------
PAGE

OVTYPE=OLA
OVNUM=OVNUM+1
OVADR=OVADR+400
OVRLCA=PROVA+OVTYPE
DL=DLOPRO+OVNUM		/DISK BLOCK OF THIS OVERLAY
FIELD OVNUM%20+3

RELOC
*OVADR
RELOC OVRLCA

	-OVNUM



/***********************************************************************/
/									/
/ THIS ENTIRE PAGE ADDED REV203						/
/									/
/ IF A JOB NEEDS THE PRINTER FIELD, IT WILL SET PRACTN=7777		
/ THE PRINTER MUST JEXIT IN RESIDENT CODE. IN THAT CASE			
/ THE OTHER JOB CLOBBERS THE PRINTER FIELD, AND WHEN DONE		
/ RELOADS THE PRINTER FIELD BY READING ONLY THE RESIDENT BLOCKS		
/ BACK IN FROM THE DISK.  WHEN THE OTHER JOB DOES THE JSTRT		
/ WE WILL START IN RESIDENT CODE AND EVERYTHING WILL BE COOL		

/ HOWEVER, SOME CRITICAL VALUES MUST BE SAVED SO THAT THEY		
/ CAN BE RESTORED LATER.  WHEN THE PRINTER IS STARTED AGAIN		
/ THE LOCATIONS 10020-10077 MUST BE RESTORED, AND SOME LOCATIONS	
/ MUST BE INITIALIZED							



/ THIS SECTION SAVES THE CRITICAL VALUES BEFORE DISPATCHING TO THE	
/ RESIDENT CODE TO GO TO SLEEP						
	OVPRJX=.-OVRLCA							
	OBPRJX=OVTYPE+OVNUM						

	DCA	PRACTN		/ SIGNAL OTHER JOB WE HAVE SEEN FLAG	
	TAD	(PRFVBF-1	/ SET UP INDEX POINTER TO SAVE BUFFER	
	DCA	X0		/ IN SYS				
	TAD	(PRSVTB-1	/ SET UP INDEX POINTER TO LIST OF	
	DCA	X1		/ ADDRESSES TO BE SAVED			
WAITJ2,	CDFMYF			/ CHANGE TO THIS FIELD			
	TAD I	X1		/ GET AN ADDRESS			
	SNA			/					
	JMP	WAITJX		/ IF ZERO SAVING IS DONE		
	DCA	T1		/ SAVE IN TEMP				
	TAD I	T1		/ GET VALUE AT THAT ADDRESS		
	CDFSYS			/ AND SAVE IN SYSTEM FIELD		
	DCA I	X0		/ IN PRFVBF BUFFER			
	JMP	WAITJ2		/ BACK TO LOOP POINT			

PRSVTB,				/ TABLE OF POINTERS TO LOCATIONS	
				/ TO SAVE WHEN BEFORE JEXIT		
	PRQPNT			/ DOCUMENT DESTINATION			
	PRQPSZ			/ PAGE SIZE OF LAST DOC PRINTED		
	PRTID			/ PRINTER ID LOCATION			
				/ OTHER JOB MUST CLEAR PRSTTS		
				/ BEFORE STARTING UP PRINTER		
	0			/ STOPPER				


	OVWKUP=.-OVRLCA		/ ENTRY POINT TO WAKE UP PRINTER
	OBWKUP=OVTYPE+OVNUM

/ RESET PRIRFD SO WE ARE SURE TO READ FROM THE FILE INTERFACE!		/A263
	DCA	PRIRFD		/ WAS BUG WPSV2-380			/A263

	DCA	PRSTTS		/ INIALIZE SOME VALUES DIRECTLY
				/ PABORT, PRSTOP, PRACTN, PRACTV ARE CLEARED
				/ AT OVWAIT
	DCA	LPONLN		/ TELL WORLD WE HAVE NO PRINTER

/ MAKE SURE WE LOAD ALL OVERLAYS AFRESH			
	AC4000			/LOAD A CODE TO MAKE SURE	
	DCA	OLA+PROVA	/WE LOAD THIS OVERLAY AFRESH	
	AC4000			/LOAD A CODE TO MAKE SURE	
	DCA	OLB+PROVA	/WE LOAD THIS OVERLAY AFRESH	
	AC4000			/LOAD A CODE TO MAKE SURE	
	DCA	OLC+PROVA	/WE LOAD THIS OVERLAY AFRESH	


/ NOW RESTORE THE CRITICAL VALUES SAVED IN THE SPECIAL BUFFER IN WPSYS

	TAD	(PRFVBF-1
	DCA	X0
	TAD	(PRSVTB-1
	DCA	X1
WAKEU2,	TAD I	X1		/ READ AN ADDRESS
	SNA
	JMP	WAKEU3		/ DONE IF ZERO
	DCA	T1
	CDFSYS
	TAD I	X0		/ READ SYS BUFFER
	CDFMYF
	DCA I	T1		/ SAVE IN CORRECT PRINTER LOCATION
	JMP	WAKEU2		/ LOOP BACK

WAKEU3,				/ DONE, GO BACK TO WAIT OVERLAY

	TAD	(OVWAIT
	JMS	PROV
		OBWAIT

/OVDDCK MUST RESIDE IN AN OVERLAY TYPE DIFFERENT THAN THE WAIT OVERLAY.	/A231
/THIS IS BECAUSE IT MAY BE CALLED BY LIST PROCESSING WHILE THE PRINT JOB
/IS IDLE (MENU GUARANTEES THIS).  BUT THE WAIT OVERLAY HAD BETTER STAY
/IN PLACE OR ALL KINDS OF HAVOC COULD (AND ONCE UPON A TIME DID) RESULT.


OVDDCK=.-OVRLCA			/PART OF PRTRID ROUTINE			/A166
OBDDCK=OVTYPE+OVNUM		/TO CHECK DOCUMENT DESTINATION		/A166
				/IF NOT GOING TO HOST			/A166
	TAD	PRQPNT		/					/A166
	TAD	(-DDP2)		/ IS IT A DRAFT PRINTER?		/A166
	SNA			/					/A166
	JMP	CHKDP		/ YES, SEE IF DRAFT PRINTER IS ONLINE	/A166
	TAD	(DDP2-DSQ1	/ SEE IF IT IS A SERIAL LQP		/A166
	SNA CLA
	JMP	CHKLQP		/ YES SEE IF LQP IS ONLINE
				/ FALL THROUGH TO ERROR ON UNRECOGNIZED	/A166
				/ DOCUMENT DESTINATION			/A166

DDERR,	ISZ	PRTRID		/ ERROR--BUMP RETURN POINT		/A166
	JMP	IDRTN		/ GO RETURN				/A166

CHKDP,	TAD	LPONLN		/					/A143
	AND	(DPMSK)		/					/A143
	SNA CLA			/ SKIP IF NOT DP ONLINE			/A143
	JMP	DDERR		/ NOT A DRAFT PRINTER, COMPLAIN		/A166
	JMS	SKIPIF		/ SKIP IF LA100 OR LA50			/C216
		DPMSK+XRPMSK	/					/A216
	JMP	IDRTN		/ NON LA100 DRAFT PRINTER IS ONLINE, EXIT


/ AT THIS POINT WE HAVE A SELECTABLE QUALITY DRAFT PRINTER ONLINE	/A166
/	 WE SELECT ITS DRAFT QUALITY MODE.				/A166
	
	JMS	PRESCS		/ SELECT DRAFT QUALITY MODE		/C216
		PCSQM		/					/A216
	JMP	IDRTN		/ LA50/100 ONLINE, EXIT			/A166

CHKLQP,	JMS	SKIPIF		/ SKIP IF LA100 OR LA50			/C216
		DPMSK+XRPMSK	/					/A216
	JMP	CHKLQ1		/ NO, SEE IF LQP			/A166

/ AT THIS POINT WE HAVE A SELECTABLE QUALITY DRAFT PRINTER ONLINE	/A166
/ WE SELECT ITS HIGHEST QUALITY MODE					/A166

	AC0002			/ ENABLE LETTER QUALITY MODE		/A166
	JMS	PRESCS		/ ENABLE LETTER QUALITY MODE		/C216
		PCSQM		/					/A166
	CLA			/ THIS WAS MISSING CAUSING PROBLEMS	/A231
	JMP	IDRTN		/ LA50/LA100 ONLINE, EXIT		/A166

CHKLQ1,		/ NON DRAFT TYPE LQP ATTACHED				/A166
	TAD	LPONLN		/ GET TYPE OF PRINTER			/A143
	SMA CLA			/ SKIP IF NOT LQP			/A143
	JMP	DDERR		/ NOT LQP, COMPLAIN			/A166
	JMP	IDRTN		/ 					/A166



XSTQM,	52; 42; 4172		/ ESC [ * " z	/ SET QUALITY MODE
/-----------------------
	PAGE

OVTYPE=OLB
OVNUM=OVNUM+1
OVADR=OVADR+400
OVRLCA=PROVA+OVTYPE
DL=DLOPRO+OVNUM		/DISK BLOCK OF THIS OVERLAY
FIELD OVNUM%20+3

RELOC
*OVADR
RELOC OVRLCA

	-OVNUM



IFDEF ENGLSH <						/A020
IFNDEF ENGCAN <						/A020
PRHPRT="P-200		/ PRINTER
PRHHDR="T-200		/ TOP
PRHHD2="T-200		/ HEADER
PRHFOT="B-200		/ BOTTOM
PRHRES="R-200		/ RESET
PRHMTC="M-200		/ MULTI
PRHSEC="S-200		/ SECTION
>	/END NDEF ENGCAN				/A020
IFDEF ENGCAN <						/A020
PRHHDR="T-200						/A020
PRHHD2="H-200						/A020
PRHFOT="B-200						/A020
PRHRES="R-200						/A020
PRHMTC="M-200						/A020
PRHSEC="S-200						/A020
>	/END ENGCAN					/A020
>	/END ENGLSH					/A020
IFDEF ITALIAN <						/A020
PRHPRT="S-200		/ PRINTER
PRHHDR="I-200						/A020
PRHHD2="I-200						/A020
PRHFOT="F-200						/A020
PRHRES="R-200						/A020
PRHMTC="M-200						/A020
PRHSEC="S-200						/A020
>	/END ITALIAN					/A020
IFDEF FRENCH <						/A020
PRHHDR="T-200						/A020
PRHHD2="H-200						/A020
PRHFOT="B-200						/A020
PRHRES="R-200						/A020
PRHMTC="M-200						/A020
PRHSEC="S-200						/A020
>	/END FRENCH					/A020
IFDEF DUTCH <						/A020
PRHPRT="P-200						/A020
PRHHDR="B-200						/A020
PRHHD2="B-200						/A020
PRHFOT="O-200						/A020
PRHRES="T-200						/A020
PRHMTC="M-200						/A020
PRHSEC="S-200						/A020
>	/END DUTCH					/A020

IFDEF V30NOR <						/A020
PRHPRT="S-200		/ PRINTER
PRHHDR="T-200		/ TOP
PRHHD2="T-200		/ HEADER
PRHFOT="B-200		/ BOTTOM
PRHRES="N-200		/ RESET
PRHMTC="M-200		/ MULTI
PRHSEC="A-200		/ SECTION
>

IFDEF V30SWE <						/A020
PRHPRT="S-200		/ PRINTER
PRHHDR="V-200		/ TOP
PRHHD2="V-200		/ HEADER
PRHFOT="N-200		/ BOTTOM
PRHRES="E-200		/ RESET
PRHMTC="M-200		/ MULTI
PRHSEC="S-200		/ SECTION
>

IFDEF SPANISH <						/A020
PRHPRT="P-200		/ PRINTER
PRHHDR="I-200		/ TOP
PRHHD2="I-200		/ HEADER
PRHFOT="F-200		/ BOTTOM
PRHRES="R-200		/ RESET
PRHMTC="M-200		/ MULTI
PRHSEC="S-200		/ SECTION
>



OVCNTL=.-OVRLCA
OBCNTL=OVTYPE+OVNUM

/THIS SECTION HANDLES PRINT CONTROL BLOCKS.  IT DETERMINES WHETHER THE
/CONTROL BLOCK IS FOR RESET, MULTI-COLUMN, HEADERS, FOOTERS, SECTION,
/OR PRINTER, AND DISPATCHES ACCORDINGLY

/THIS LOOP FINDS THE FIRST NON-BLANK, NON-CONTROL CHARACTER IN THE BLOCK.
/THIS IDENTIFIES THE TYPE OF BLOCK.


PRHIST,
	JMS	PRGCHC	/GET A CHARACTER
	JMP	PRSTR1	/END OF CONTROL BLOCK				/C225

	AND	P177	/STRIP THE MODE BITS
	TAD	(-40)	/IS IT A TEXT CHARACTER?
	SPA SNA
	JMP	PRHIST	/NO--KEEP LOOKING

/THE FIRST CHARACTER OF THE COMMAND BLOCK HAS BEEN FOUND AND IS IN THE AC.
/CONVERT IT TO LOWER CASE IF NECESSARY.


	TAD   (40-140)	/CONVERT TO LOWER CASE
	SMA
	TAD	(-40)

/NOW DETERMINE WHAT KIND OF COMMAND IT IS.

	TAD (140-PRHMTC)	/MULTI-COLUMN?
	SNA
	JMP	PRMLTC		/YES

	TAD (PRHMTC-PRHHDR)	/HEADER?
	SNA
	JMP	PRHEAD		/YES

	TAD (PRHHDR-PRHHD2)	/SECOND HEADER?			/A020
	SNA							/A020
	JMP	PRHEAD		/YES

	TAD (PRHHD2-PRHFOT)	/FOOTER?			/M020
	SNA
	JMP	PRFOOT		/YES

	TAD (PRHFOT-PRHRES)	/RESET?
	SNA
	JMP	PRRSET		/YES

	TAD (PRHRES-PRHSEC)	/SECTION?
	SNA
	JMP	PRSECT		/YES

	TAD	(PRHSEC-PRHPRT)	/PRINTER?
	SNA
	JMP	PRPRNT		/YES

/THE COMMAND BLOCK IS INVALID.  STRIP THE REST OF THE BLOCK AND RETURN.

	CLA
	JMP	PRSTRP	/RETURN


/ HERE WE JUMP TO THE OVERLAY TO HANDLE PRINTER CONTROL STATEMENTS	/A168

PRPRNT,	TAD	(OVPRNT)
	JMS	PROV
		OBPRNT

/THS SECTION HANDLES A MULTI-COLUMN CONTROL BLOCK.  IF WE ARE NOT PRINTING TO
/AN LQP, WE CANNOT HAVE MULTI-COLUMNS.

PRMLTC,
	TAD	LPONLN	/SEE WHAT KIND OF PRINTER WE HAVE		/A143
	AND	(MCMSK)							/A143
	SZA CLA		/SKIP IF NOT LQP			/C143	/C148
	JMP	PRMCL0	/AN LQP--PROCESS THE BLOCK

	JMP	PRSTRP	/STRIP THE REST OF THE BLOCK AND RETURN

/NOW, WE ZERO OUT THE MULTI-COLUMN INFORMATION ARRAY.  THIS ARRAY CONTAINS
/THE NUMBER OF COLUMNS, FOLLOWED BY THE WIDTH OF SUCCESSIVE COLUMNS.

PRMCL0,
	TAD (-PRMCAC-1)	/GET THE NUMBER OF ENTRIES IN THE TABLE
	DCA	T1
	TAD (PRMCAY-1)	/GET ONE BEFORE THE BEGINNING OF THE TABLE
	DCA	X0

/THIS LOOP INITIALIZES THE NUMBER OF COLUMNS TO ONE, AND CLEARS THE COLUMN
/WIDTH ENTRIES.

	AC0001		/PUT A ONE IN THE FIRST LOCATION

PRMCL1,
	DCA I	X0	/DEPOSIT THE VALUE
	ISZ	T1	/ANY MORE TABLE POSITIONS?
	JMP	PRMCL1	/CONTINUE THE LOOP


/NOW, WE READ THE MULTI-COLUMN PRINTER CONTROL BLOCK, USING THE VALUES
/TO INITIALIZE THE ARRAY.  PRMCGN RETURNS THE SUCCESSIVE NUMBER FROM
/THE BLOCK.  WHEN THE END PRINT CONTROL IS ENCOUNTERED, PRMCGN
/MAKES A NORMAL RETURN; OTHERWISE, IT MAKES A SKIP RETURN.

/FIRST, WE READ AND RECORD THE NUMBER OF COLUMNS, AND TEST TO SEE IF
/THAT NUMBER EXCEEDS THE PERMISSIBLE MAXIMUM.

	JMS	PRMCGN	/GET THE FIRST NUMBER
	JMP	PRMCL4	/EOC ENCOUNTERED--NOTHING MORE TO READ

/IF THE NUMBER OF COLUMNS IS SPECIFIED TO BE ZERO OR LESS, USE ONE INSTEAD.

	SPA SNA
	AC0001
	DCA	PRMCAY	/SAVE THE NUMBER OF COLUMNS

/NOW, IF THE SPECIFIED NUMBER OF COLUMNS EXCEEDS THE MAXIMUM, THE NUMBER IS
/REDUCED TO THE MAXIMUM.

	TAD	PRMCAY	/GET THE NUMBER OF COLUMNS
	TAD  (-PRMCAC)	/SUBTRACT THE MAXIMUM NUMBER OF ENTRIES
	SPA CLA
	JMP	PRMCL2	/THE NUMBER OF COLUMNS IS IN RANGE

/THE CONTROL BLOCK SPECIFIED MORE COLUMNS THAN CAN BE TREATED WITH THE
/ARRAY--CHANGE THE NUMBER OF COLUMNS TO THE MAXIMUM.

	TAD   (PRMCAC)	/GET MAXIMUM NUMBER OF ENTRIES
	DCA	PRMCAY	/SAVE AS THE NUMBER OF COLUMNS

/WE NOW HAVE A WORKABLE NUMBER OF COLUMNS.  THIS LOOP READS NUMBERS FROM THE
/CONTROL BLOCK UNTIL EITHER THE TABLE IS FILLED OR THE BLOCK IS EXHAUSTED.

PRMCL2,
	TAD  (-PRMCAC)	/GET MAXIMUM NUMBER OF ENTRIES
	DCA	T3	/SAVE IN A COUNTER

	TAD   (PRMCAY)	/GET STARTING ADDRESS OF ARRAY
	DCA	X0	/SAVE IN INDEX LOCATION

/READ THE COLUMN WIDTH INFORMATION INTO THE ARRAY.

PRMCL3,
	JMS	PRMCGN	/GET ANOTHER NUMBER
	JMP	PRMCL4	/END OF COMMAND BLOCK--NO MORE TO READ
	DCA I	X0	/STORE IN THE ARRAY
	ISZ	T3	/IS THERE ROOM FOR MORE?
	JMP	PRMCL3	/YES--CONTINUE THE LOOP

/WE HAVE READ THE CONTROL BLOCK AND RE-INITIALIZED THE ARRAY.  WE NOW MUST
/PERFORM NECESSARY FORMATTING TO MAKE ROOM FOR THE COLUMNS.

/IF WE ARE NOT CURRENTLY PRINTING ON ANY COLUMN, WE MERELY RETURN.  OTHERWISE,
/WE SET THE NEW COLUMN REQUEST FLAG BEFORE RETURNING SO THAT THE SPACING
/ROUTINE CAN SET THEM UP.

PRMCL4,
	TAD	PRMCPC	/GET THE CURRENT COLUMN NUMBER
	SZA CLA
	AC7777		/SET THE REQUEST FLAG
	DCA	PRMCRF	/THE FLAG IS SET OR CLEARED DEPENDING ON WHETHER
			/WE ARE CURRENTLY PRINTING A COLUMN OR NOT

	JMP	PRSTRP	/GO STRIP, RESTORE READCH, ETC.		/D188	/A192

/THIS SECTION READS THE NEXT NUMBER WHICH IT CAN FIND IN THE PRINT CONTROL
/BLOCK.  A NUMBER IS A STRING OF DIGITS TERMINATED BY A NON-DIGIT.

PRMCGN,
	XX

/WE INITIALIZE THE VARIABLES.  PRMCFF WILL REMAIN NEGATIVE UNTIL A DIGIT
/IS LOCATED.

	AC7777
	DCA	PRMCFF	/SET THE FOUND SOMETHING FLAG TO FOUND NOTHING
	DCA	PRMCGT	/CLEAR THE NUMBER TEMPORARY

/THIS LOOP READS CHARACTERS FROM THE CONTROL BLOCK AND TESTS THEM AS TO
/WHETHER THEY ARE DIGITS.

PRMCG1,
	JMS	PRGCHC	/GET THE NEXT CHARACTER				/C192
	JMP I	PRMCGN	/END OF CONTROL--RETURN
	AND	P177	/STRIP THE MODE BITS

/THE ASCII REPRESENTATION OF A DIGIT MUST LIE BETWEEN 60 AND 71, INCLUSIVE.
/THIS SECTION CHECKS FOR A VALID DIGIT.

	JMS	DIGCHK	/SEE IF A DIGIT					/A145
	JMP	PRMCG2	/NO--JUMP TO HANDLE IT				/A145

/THE AC NOW CONTAINS THE OCTAL VALUE CORRESPONDING TO THE DECIMAL DIGIT JUST
/FOUND.  WE ADD THIS TO THE NUMBER ALREADY PARTIALLY READ AND CONTINUE.

	DCA	PRMCFF	/SAVE THE DIGIT

	TAD	PRMCGT
	JMS	PRMULT	/MULTIPLY THE PARTIALLY READ NUMBER BY 10
		(12)

	TAD	PRMCFF	/ADD IN THE NEW DIGIT
	DCA	PRMCGT	/SAVE AS THE PARTIALLY READ NUMBER
	JMP	PRMCG1	/LOOK FOR THE NEXT DIGIT


/THIS SECTION IS INVOKED WHEN A NON-DIGIT CHARACTER IS READ.  IF WE ARE AT
/THE END OF THE CONTROL BLOCK, THE ZERO IS RETURNED.  OTHERWISE,
/THE VALUE IN PRMCFF IS TESTED.  IF IT IS STILL NEGATIVE, NO DIGITS HAVE BEEN
/FOUND YET, AND WE KEEP LOOKING.  IF IT IS NOW NON-NEGATIVE, WE HAVE FOUND
/A NUMBER AND THIS CHARACTER TERMINATES IT.  RETURN THE NUMBER.

/THIS SECTION IS INVOKED WHEN A NON-DIGIT CHARACTER IS READ.  THE VALUE OF
/PRMCFF IS TESTED.  IF IT IS STILL NEGATIVE, NO DIGITS HAVE BEEN FOUND
/YET, AND WE KEEP LOOKING.  IF IT IS NOW NON-NEGATIVE, WE HAVE FOUND A
/NUMBER AND THIS CHARACTER TERMINATES IT.  RETURN THE NUMBER.

PRMCG2,
	CLA

	TAD	PRMCFF	/SEE IF A NUMBER WAS FOUND
	SPA CLA
	JMP	PRMCG1	/NO--KEEP LOOKING

	TAD	PRMCGT	/YES--RETURN THE NUMBER
	ISZ	PRMCGN
	JMP I	PRMCGN

PRMCGT,	0	/THE DIGITS ARE COMBINED INTO A NUMBER HERE
PRMCFF,	0	/THIS FLAG IS NEGATIVE UNTIL A DIGIT IS FOUND, IS POSITIVE
		/THERAFTER.  ALSO SERVES AS A TEMPORARY.


PAGE

/THIS SECTION HANDLES A RESET (PAGE NUMBER) BLOCK.  IT DETERMINES
/IF TWO SUCESSIVE EVEN OR ODD PAGES IN A ROW ARE CAUSED BY THE RESET
/COMMAND.  IF SO, AND WE ARE PRINTING EO, WE WILL OUTPUT A PAGE INSERT
/COMMAND AT OVTOPP.

PRRSET,
	TAD	PRQPGO		/GET THE IP PAGE SETTING
	DCA	PNRSVL		/SAVE AS PAGE NUMBER RESET VALUE	/A260
	JMS	PRMCGN		/IS THERE A NUMBER SPECIFIED?
	SZA
	DCA	PNRSVL		/YES, SAVE THIS VALUE INSTEAD
	TAD	PNRSVL		/NOW EXCLUSIVE OR THE LEAST SIGNFICANT BIT
	TAD	PRPGNM		/SIMPLY BY ADDING THE TWO VALUES
	RAR			/SAVE IN LINK				/A260
	CLA			/CLEAR AC				/A260
	TAD	PNRSVL		/ACTUALLY RESET THE PAGE NUMBER		/A260
	DCA	PRPGNM		/					/A260
	RAL			/GET THE LINK				/A260
	DCA	PNRSVL		/SAVE IN THE PAGE NUMBER RESET LOCATION	/A260
	JMP	PRSTRP		/STRIP THE REST OF THE BLOCK AND RETURN


/THIS SECTION HANDLES A SECTION BLOCK.  IT INCREMENTS THE SECTION NUMBER,
/UNLESS ONE IS SPECIFIED IN THE BLOCK.

PRSECT,
	ISZ	PRSCNM	/INCREMENT THE SECTION NUMBER
	JMS	PRMCGN	/IS THERE A NUMBER SPECIFIED?
	SZA
	DCA	PRSCNM	/YES--SET THE SECTION NUMBER TO THE VALUE
	JMP	PRSTRP	/STRIP THE REST OF THE BLOCK AND RETURN


/THIS SECTION STRIPS THE REST OF THE CHARACTERS FROM THE CONTROL BLOCK, RESETS
/THE READ LINK, AND RETURNS TO THE GET CHARACTER ROUTINE.

OVSTRP=.-OVRLCA
OBSTRP=OVTYPE+OVNUM

PRSTRP,
	TAD	T1		/ ARE WE ALREADY AT EOC?		/A192
	TAD	(-EPC		/					/A192
	SNA CLA			/					/A192
	JMP	PRSTR1		/ YES					/A192
PRSTR2,	JMS	PTSTST		/ SEE IF USER WANTS TO STOP		/A182
	JMS	PRGCHC		/GET A CHARACTER			/C192
	JMP	PRSTR1		/END OF CONTROL FOUND			/C192
	CLA								/A192
	JMP	PRSTR2		/KEEP STRIPPING				/A192

/WE ARE AT THE END OF THE PRINT CONTROL					/C188
/RESET THE READ LINK
/IT WAS CHANGED WHEN THE END OF THE PRINT CONTROL WAS FOUND		/C188
/IT ALSO MAY BE NON ZERO IF PROCESSING LIST PROCESSING HEADERS/FOOTERS	/A188

	OVEXPC=.-OVRLCA							/A173
	OBEXPC=OVTYPE+OVNUM						/A173
PRSTR1,

	TAD	LPFLAG		/ARE WE LIST PROCESSING?
	SNA CLA
	TAD	(RDFILE-RDEDIT)
	TAD	(RDEDIT)
	DCA	RDCHAR		/APPROPRIATELY RESET READ ROUTINE PTR

	JMP	PRGCH1		/GO GET NEXT CHR AS A TEXT CHR


/THIS SECTION HANDLES HEADER BLOCKS.

PRHEAD,
	AC0001		/PREPARE TO DO A HEADER MEASUREMENT
	JMS	SIZE	/GET THE SIZE OF THE HEADER
	DCA	PRHSIZ	/SAVE IT
	JMP	PRSTR1	/STRIP THE REST OF THE BLOCK AND RETURN		/C185

/THIS SECTION HANDLES FOOTER BLOCKS.

PRFOOT,
	AC7777		/PREPARE TO DO A FOOTER MEASUREMENT
	JMS	SIZE	/GET THE SIZE OF THE FOOTER
	DCA	PRFSIZ	/SAVE IT
	JMP	PRSTR1	/STRIP THE REST OF THE BLOCK AND RETURN		/C185


/THIS ROUTINE RETURNS THE SIZE OF THE HEADER/FOOTER BLOCK, HAVING SAVED
/ITS LOCATION AND STRIPPED IT FROM THE FILE.

SIZE,
	XX

	DCA	SIZET1	/SAVE THE TYPE OF SAVE THIS IS

	JMS	PROV	/LOAD THE SAVE/RESTORE OVERLAY
		OBHIGS	/BUT DO NOT GO THERE				/C185

	JMS	PRHIGS	/STRIP THE REST OF THE LINE
	NOP		/WON'T FIND EOC

	TAD	SIZET1	/RECOVER THE ARGUMENT
	JMS	PRSAVE	/SAVE THE CRUCIAL INFORMATION ABOUT THE HEADER/FOOTER


/WE NOW READ THE LINES OF THE BLOCK.  EACH TIME A NEW LINE IS FOUND, THE
/SIZE OF THE BLOCK IS UPDATED.  IF END OF CONTROL IS SEEN WE RETURN TO	/C185
/THE NO-- LINE.  IF END OF FILE IS SEEN WE WILL NOT RETURN HERE BUT	/A185
/REPORT MISSING CONTROL FROM PRHIGS					/A185


	DCA	SIZET1	/CLEAR THE SIZE TEMPORARY

SIZE1,
	JMS	PRHIGS	/IS THERE ANOTHER LINE?
	JMP	SIZE2	/NO--PREPARE TO RETURN
	TAD	PRLLSZ	/YES--GET LINE SIZE
	TAD	SIZET1	/ADD TO THE BLOCK SIZE
	DCA	SIZET1	/SAVE AS THE NEW BLOCK SIZE
	JMP	SIZE1	/CONTINUE WITH THE LOOP

/WE PREPARE TO RETURN.

SIZE2,
	DCA	PRFLAG	/SHOW REGULAR TEXT IS PRINTING
	JMS	PRTRNC	/GET THE PROPER SPACING FOR REGULAR TEXT
	TAD	SIZET1	/RECOVER THE BLOCK SIZE
	JMP I	SIZE	/AND RETURN


SIZET1,	0	/A TEMPORARY FOR THE SIZE ROUTINE



/-------------------
PAGE

